window.__GNAMMA_SANANDAJI84.push(...[{"id":"I214.lower.06","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بأن يختلف باختلافه الخ) قال بعضهم إن قيد التميز في المسموع\nللاحتراز من مثل الطول والقصر لأن معناه أن يكون ما به التميز مسموعا وهما ليسا من\nالمسموعات واعترض بأنهما ليسا من الكيفيات مطلقا فالأولى أنه للاحتراز من مثل\nالغنة والبحوحة ثم لما رأوا في قيد الحدة والثقل من المسموعات دون الغنة والبحوحة\nنظرا قالوا وتبعهم الشارح مد ظله إن معنى التميز المذكور ليس ما ذكر\nبل معناه أن يحصل به التميز في نفس المسموع بأن يختلف باختلافه ويتحد باتحاده","english":"On “It differs when the quality differs, etc.” Some said that “distinction in what is heard” excludes such things as length and brevity: it means that the feature distinguishing the sound is audible, whereas those two are not. The objection is that they are not qualities at all; it is therefore preferable that the restriction exclude such things as nasal resonance and hoarseness. Then, seeing a difficulty in counting high and low pitch among audible features but not nasal resonance and hoarseness, they said—and the commentator, may his shade endure, followed them—that the distinction does not mean what was just stated. Rather, the quality produces a distinction in the audible object itself, which differs when it differs and is identical when it is identical—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I215.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I214.upper.01","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وما ذلك إلا لمنع الأنبوبة وصول الهواء إلى صماخ غيره (وإذا رجع) الصوت (لمصادمة\nجسم أملس) فإن ذلك الجسم يقاوم الصوت ويصرفه إلى خلف (فهو) أي فذلك\nالصوت الراجع (الصدى","english":"This is only because the tube prevents the air from reaching anyone else’s auditory passage. “When” sound “returns through striking a smooth body”—the body resists the sound and turns it back—“it,” the returning sound, “is the echo.”","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I214.upper.02","volume":1,"page":214,"physical_pdf_page":227,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وإذا عرض له) أي الصوت (كيفية بها يمتاز)\nالصوت المعروض للكيفية (عما) أي عن صوت آخر (يماثله في الحدة)\nأي الزيرية (والثقل) أي البمية احترازا عن الحدة والثقل فإنهما وإن كانتا\nكيفيتين عارضتين للصوت فيمتاز بهما ذلك الصوت عما يخالفه في تلك الكيفية\nالعارضة إلا أنه لا يمتاز بالحدة صوت عن آخر يماثله في الحدة ولا بالثقل صوت عما\nيشاركه فيه (تميزا في نفس المسموع) بأن يختلف باختلافه ويتحد\nباتحاده كما في الحروف احترازا عما ليس كذلك كالغنة والبحوحة إذ قد يختلف","english":"“If a quality occurs in it”—sound—“through which it differs from another” sound “equal to it in high” pitch, zīriyya, “and low” pitch, bamiyya, [then it is a phoneme, as the next page concludes]. This excludes pitch itself. High and low pitch are indeed qualities occurring in sound, by which a sound differs from one unlike it in that quality. But high pitch does not distinguish a sound from another equally high-pitched sound, nor low pitch from one sharing its low pitch. The distinction must be “in the audible object itself”: it differs when the quality differs and is identical when the quality is identical, as with phonemes. This excludes features unlike that, such as nasal resonance and hoarseness, since—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I215.upper.01","editorial":"Ḥarf is rendered “phoneme” in this acoustical definition, while the familiar written letter names are preserved in the examples. It is not claimed that the medieval definition coincides with all aspects of modern phonology.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.lower.01","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"دفعا لذلك النظر لكن أقول إذا كان المعنى هذا لم يحتج في الحدة والثقل إلى قيد\nالمماثلة المذكورة فتدبر جدا","english":"[This explanation] removes that objection. But I say: if this is the meaning, the stated restriction of equality in high and low pitch is unnecessary. Consider this very carefully.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I214.lower.06","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.lower.02","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فذلك الصوت المعروض الخ) قيل وفي عبارة\nابن سينا ما يدل على أن الحرف هو الكيفية العارضة","english":"On “That qualified sound, etc.” It is said that Ibn Sīnā’s wording indicates that the phoneme is the occurring quality itself.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.lower.03","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مجموع العارض\nوالمعروض الخ) وكأنه الأشبه بالحق كما قال بعض المحققين","english":"On “The combination of the quality and its subject, etc.” This seems closer to the truth, as some investigators have said.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.lower.04","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهي الحركات\nالحاصلة من إمالة الخ) ليس المراد من الحركة ههنا ما هو من خواص الأجسام بل هي\nعبارة عن كيفية حاصلة في الحرف الصامت من إمالة المخرج إلى مخرج إحدى المدات الخ\nكما في شرح المقاصد فظهر أن في عبارة الشارح مد ظله خللا لا يخفى فليتأمل","english":"On “The vowel-movements produced by directing, etc.” “Movement” here does not mean the property of bodies. It means a quality produced in a consonantal sound through directing its articulation towards that of a lengthened vowel, and so forth, as in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. There is therefore an evident defect in the commentator’s expression—may his shade endure. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.lower.05","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله متولدة من إشباع ما قبلها الخ) قالوا لا خلاف في امتناع الابتداء بهذه المدات\nوإنما الخلاف في أن ذلك لسكونها حتى يمتنع الابتداء بالساكن الصامت أيضا أو لكونها\nعبارة عن مدة متولدة من إشباع الحركة المجانسة فلا تتصور إلا حيث يكون قبلها\nصامت متحرك قال في شرح المقاصد وهذا هو الحق لأن كل سليم الحس يجوز إمكان\nالابتداء بالساكن وإن كان مرفوضا في لغة العرب","english":"On “Produced by prolonging the preceding vowel, etc.” They say there is no disagreement that speech cannot begin with these lengthened vowels. The disagreement is whether this is because they are unvowelled, so that an unvowelled consonant likewise cannot begin speech, or because they are a prolongation produced by extending the corresponding vowel-movement, so that they are inconceivable without a preceding vowelled consonant. Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says that the latter is correct: anyone with sound sense admits the possibility of beginning with an unvowelled consonant, even though Arabic usage rejects it.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.lower.06","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهو ما سوى المصوت الخ)\nفيندرج فيه الواو والياء المتحركتان أو الساكنتان إذا لم يكن قبل الواو ضمة وقبل الياء\nكسرة وأما الألف فلا تكون إلا مصوتا وإطلاقها على الهمزة باشتراك الاسم","english":"On “What is other than the vocalic, etc.” This includes wāw and yāʾ when vowelled, or when unvowelled without a preceding ḍamma for wāw or kasra for yāʾ. Alif, however, is only vocalic; applying its name to hamza is a case of homonymy.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.upper.01","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الصوت فيهما والمسموع واحد وقد يتحد والمسموع مختلف (فهو) أي فذلك\nالصوت المعروض لتلك الكيفية (الحرف) وجنح على أنه مجموع العارض\nوالمعروض","english":"[Nasal resonance and hoarseness] may differ in the sound while the audible object is one; conversely, they may be identical while the audible object differs. “It”—the sound qualified by that quality—“is the phoneme.” He inclines to its being the combination of the occurring quality and its subject.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I214.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.upper.02","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وينقسم إلى مصوت) وهو إما (مقصور وهي الحركات)\nالحاصلة من إمالة المخرج إلى مخرج إحدى المدات فإلى ألف الفتحة وإلى ياء الكسرة\nوإلى واو الضمة (أو ممدود وهي المدات) وهي الألف والواو والياء إذا كانت\nساكنة متولدة من إشباع ما قبلها من الحركات المجانسة لها","english":"“It is divided into a vocalic sound,” either “short—the vowel-movements” produced by directing the articulation towards that of one of the lengthened vowels: towards alif for fatḥa, yāʾ for kasra, and wāw for ḍamma; “or long—the lengthened vowels,” namely alif, wāw and yāʾ when unvowelled and produced by prolonging the preceding vowel-movement of the corresponding kind.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I215.upper.03","volume":1,"page":215,"physical_pdf_page":228,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) إلى (صامت) وهو\nما سوى المصوت ودعيا بالمصوت والصامت لتعذر التكلم أو تعسره بدون الحركات\nوالمدات والصمت السكوت فإطلاقهما عليهما تجوزا (والصامت مع) المصوت","english":"It is also divided into “a consonantal sound,” what is other than the vocalic. They are called voiced and silent because speech is impossible or difficult without the vowel-movements and lengthened vowels. Silence means being mute; applying the terms to these two is therefore figurative. “A consonantal sound together with” a vocalic sound—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I216.upper.01","editorial":"Ṣāmit is literally “silent”, not “voiceless” in the modern phonetic classification. The translation does not confuse these uses.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.lower.01","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مطلقا) أي سواء كانت مقاطع أو لا","english":"On “Without restriction.” Whether or not the phonemes constitute segments.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.lower.02","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله قم الخ) ومثل ق أمرا","english":"On “Qum, etc.” Likewise qi, as an imperative [“Protect!”].","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.lower.03","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويلزم\nحينئذ أن لا يكون الخ) إلا أن يعتبر معه الضمير المستتر الذي هو فاعله أعني أنت","english":"On “It then follows that it would not be, etc.” Unless the implicit pronoun which is its subject—namely anta, “you”—is counted along with it.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله يسمى مقطعا) في القاموس قطعه كمنعه قطعا ومقطعا أبانه فعلى هذا كان القطع\nمصدرا سمي به الصامت المذكور اه منه","english":"On “Is called a segment.” Al-Qāmūs gives qaṭaʿahu, inflected like manaʿahu, with the verbal nouns qaṭʿ and maqṭaʿ, meaning “separated it.” On this basis, the term of cutting-off is a verbal noun used to name the consonantal unit just mentioned. End. From him.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The lexical note is translated without replacing its account with a modern etymological claim.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.upper.01","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(المقصور يسمى مقطعا مقصورا ومع) المصوت (الممدود) يسمى مقطعا\n(ممدودا مثل لام) بالحركات الثلاث (ولا) ولي ولو","english":"[A consonant together with] a “short” vocalic sound “is called a short segment; with a long” vocalic sound it is called a “long segment. For example, lām” with each of the three short vowels—la, li, lu—and “lā,” lī and lū.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I215.upper.03","editorial":"Maqṭaʿ is rendered “segment” here; the source’s own lexical note follows separately. The transliterated examples distinguish short and long vowels.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.upper.02","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والمؤلف منها) أي من الحروف\nمطلقا (يسمى باسم الكلام وباسم اللفظ وقد يخص الكلام بما يفيد)\nفائدة يصح السكوت عليها سواء كانت النسبة فيها إنشائية كقم أو إخبارية كزيد\nقائم (وقد يخص اللفظ بما يتألف من المقاطع) ويلزم حينئذ أن لا يكون مثل\nقم من اللفظ","english":"“What is composed of them”—of phonemes without restriction—“is called speech and utterance. Speech may be restricted to what conveys” a complete benefit upon which one may fall silent, whether its relation is performative, as in “Stand!”, or informative, as in “Zayd is standing.” “Utterance may be restricted to what is composed of segments.” It then follows that an expression such as qum, “Stand!”, would not be an utterance.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.upper.03","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقد يتوهم أن اللفظ من مقولة الكم إذ تقدر جملته بجزئه)\nفإن أجزاء الأقاويل مقاطع مقصورة أو ممدودة يقع فيها التركيب والقول ربما\nيقدر بواحد منها وربما يحتاج إلى أن يقدر باثنين أو أكثر كسائر المقادير فإن\nمنها ما يقدر بذراع ومنها ما يحتاج إلى ذراعين فأكثر وكل ما هو كذلك فهو كم\n(ورد بأنه) أي التقدير ليس بالذات بل (بالعرض) لأنه من جهة الكثرة التي\nفيه كما أن الجسم يقدر بالذراع المأخوذ من الكم المتصل وإنما يكون كما لو كان التقدير\nفيه بالذات","english":"“One may suppose that utterance belongs to the category of quantity, since its whole is measured by its part.” Expressions consist of short or long segments in composition. An expression may be measured by one, or require two or more, like other magnitudes: some are measured by one cubit, others require two or more. Everything like that is a quantity. “The reply is that” the measurement is not intrinsic but “incidental”: it concerns the multiplicity in the expression. Likewise, a body is measured by the cubit taken from continuous quantity. It would itself be a quantity only if its measurement were intrinsic.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I216.upper.04","volume":1,"page":216,"physical_pdf_page":229,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"النوع الرابع من الكيفيات المحسوسة المذوقات ولاختصار\nالكلام فيها أخرها عن المبصرات والمسموعات مع أن الأولى جعلها رديفة الملموسات\nلأن إدراك القوة الذائقة مشروط باللمس (وأصول المذوقات) أي بسائطها","english":"The fourth type of sensible quality consists of tastes. Because the discussion of them is brief, he places them after visible and audible qualities, although it would be preferable to put them immediately after tangible qualities: the gustatory faculty’s apprehension requires contact. “The basic tastes”—their simples—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I217.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I217.lower.01","volume":1,"page":217,"physical_pdf_page":230,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من ضرب الثلاثة في الثلاثة الخ) ثم يتركب من تلك البسائط التسعة التي هي\nالحلاوة والمرارة والحموضة والملوحة والحرافة والعفوصة والقبض والدسومة والتفاهة\nطعوم لا تحصى بحسب اختلاف التركيب ومراتب البسائط قوة وضعفا ثم المركب قد\nيكون له اسم كالبشاعة المركب من المرارة والملوحة وقد لا يكون كالحلاوة والحرافة في\nالعسل المطبوخ والمرارة والتفاهة في الهندباء والمرارة والحرافة والقبض في الباذنجان\nلكن ينبغي أن يعلم أن التفاهة المعدودة من الطعوم ليست بمعنى عدم الطعم وإن كان\nقد يقال التفه لما لا طعم له أصلا كالماء","english":"On “Multiplying the three by the three, etc.” Innumerable tastes are then compounded from these nine simples—sweetness, bitterness, sourness, saltiness, pungency, puckering astringency, astringency, oiliness and blandness—according to differences in composition and the simples’ degrees of strength and weakness. A compound may have a name, such as harshness, compounded of bitterness and saltiness; or it may not, as with sweetness and pungency in boiled honey, bitterness and blandness in chicory, and bitterness, pungency and astringency in aubergine. It must be understood that the blandness counted among tastes does not mean absence of taste, although “tasteless” may be said of what has no taste at all, such as water.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I217.lower.02","volume":1,"page":217,"physical_pdf_page":230,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"والفرق بين القبض والعفوصة هو أن\nالقبض ما يحصل لظاهر اللسان والعفوصة لظاهره وباطنه أيضا والتفصيل موكول إلى\nالمطولات","english":"Astringency differs from puckering astringency in that the former affects the surface of the tongue, while the latter affects both its surface and its interior. Details are left to the longer works.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I217.lower.03","volume":1,"page":217,"physical_pdf_page":230,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وأنواعها غير مضبوطة الخ) وأنواعها بالتشبيه الضار ليرجع","english":"On “Their kinds are not fixed in number, etc.” Their kinds [are described] by comparison [the intervening word is uncertain], so that the account refers—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I218.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"بالتشبيه الضار","reading":"بالتشبيه الضار","status":"Compressed phrase needs enlarged reading; do not turn this into a claim about harmful odours."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I217.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":217,"physical_pdf_page":230,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لا بد له من فاعل هو الحرارة) فالحرارة إن فعلت في اللطيف حدثت الحرارة\nوفي الكثيف حدثت المرارة وفي المعتدل حدثت الملوحة والبرودة إن فعلت في\nاللطيف حدثت الحموضة وفي الكثيف العفوصة وفي المعتدل القبض والكيفية\nالمتوسطة بين الحرارة والبرودة إن فعلت في اللطيف حدثت الدسومة وفي الكثيف\nالحلاوة وفي المعتدل التفاهة وهي على نوعين أحدهما أن لا يكون له طعم أصلا\nوالثاني أن لا يكون له طعم في الحس ويكون له طعم في الحقيقة لكنه لشدة التحام\nبين أجزائه لا يتحلل منه شيء يخالط اللسان فلا يحس منه بطعم ثم إذا احتيل في\nتحليل أجزائه أحس منه بطعم وهذه هي المعدودة في الطعوم دون الأولى اه منه","english":"On “It requires an agent, which is heat.” If heat acts on the subtle, pungency results; on the dense, bitterness; on the balanced, saltiness. If cold acts on the subtle, sourness results; on the dense, puckering astringency; on the balanced, astringency. If the intermediate quality between heat and cold acts on the subtle, oiliness results; on the dense, sweetness; on the balanced, blandness. The last has two kinds. One has no taste at all. The other has no taste for sensation but does in reality: because its parts cohere strongly, none dissolves to mingle with the tongue, and no taste is sensed. When a means is found to dissolve its parts, a taste is sensed. The latter, not the former, is counted among tastes. End. From him.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"حدثت الحرارة","reading":"حدثت الحرافة","status":"The visible letterform resembles الحرارة; the intended taste is الحرافة, pungency, consistent with the ensuing inventory. Verify the final consonant before treating it as a diplomatic reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I217.upper.01","volume":1,"page":217,"physical_pdf_page":230,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(الطعوم التسعة) لأن الطعم لا بد له من فاعل هو الحرارة أو البرودة أو الكيفية\nالمتوسطة بينهما ومن قابل هو الكثيف أو اللطيف أو المتوسط بينهما والحاصل\nمن ضرب الثلاثة في الثلاثة ذلك","english":"“The nine tastes.” Taste requires an agent—heat, cold, or the intermediate quality—and a recipient—the dense, the subtle, or what is intermediate between them. Multiplying the three by the three gives this number.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I216.upper.04","editorial":"This is the historical physical theory being translated, not a modern scientific or medical assertion.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I217.upper.02","volume":1,"page":217,"physical_pdf_page":230,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) الخامس من المحسوسات (المشمومات)\nوهي (الروائح وأنواعها غير مضبوطة) وضروبها في الشدة والضعف غير منحصرة","english":"The fifth division of sensible qualities is “objects of smell,” namely “odours, whose kinds are not fixed in number.” Their varieties of intensity and weakness are not limited.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I217.upper.03","volume":1,"page":217,"physical_pdf_page":230,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"القسم (الثاني) من مطلق الكيف (الكيفيات النفسانية) المختصة","english":"The “second” division of quality in general is “psychic qualities,” specific—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I218.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.lower.01","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إلى المذوقات والمشمومات لا إلى المشمومات فقط","english":"[The account refers] to objects of taste and smell, not only to objects of smell.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I217.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.lower.02","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ثم اعلم أن الأنواع الخمسة من\nالكيفيات وإن شاع بين القوم إجراء المشتقات والصفات أعني المبصرات والملموسات\nوالمسموعات الخ عليها بحيث جعلت بمنزلة الأسماء لها لكنها بحسب اللغة متفاوتة في وقوع\nتلك الصفات عليها فتقع في البعض على المحل دون الكيفية كاللمس فيقال لمست الحجر\nولا يقال لمست لينه وفي بعض آخر بالعكس كالسمع فيقال سمعت الصوت ولا يقال\nسمعت المصوت وفي بعض آخر في المحل والكيفية كليهما كالمرئي فيقال أبصرت\nالورد وحمرته وشممت العنبر ورائحته وذقت الطعام وحلاوته","english":"Know that, although the learned commonly apply derivative descriptions—visible, tangible, audible, and so forth—to the five types of sensible quality, treating them as names for those types, linguistic usage differs. For some, the description applies to the subject, not the quality: with touch one says, “I touched the stone,” not “I touched its softness.” For others the reverse holds: with hearing one says, “I heard the sound,” not “I heard the sound-producer.” For others it applies to both subject and quality: one says, “I saw the rose and its redness,” “I smelled the ambergris and its scent,” and “I tasted the food and its sweetness.”","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.lower.03","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أن تلك الكيفيات\nتوجد في الحيوان الخ) يعني أن نسبتها إلى ذوات الأنفس ليست لكونها عارضة\nلها لاشتراك سائر العوارض لها في ذلك بل المقصود من ذلك اختصاصها بها لكن\nاختصاصها إضافيا لا حقيقيا","english":"On “These qualities occur in animals, etc.” Their attribution to possessors of souls is not because they occur as accidents in them, since other accidents share that feature. What is intended is their specificity to them—but relative, not absolute specificity.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.lower.04","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله على أن القائل بثبوتها له تعالى الخ) بل القائل\nبثبوتها للمجردات مطلقا كذلك فإن الحياة مثلا في الواجب وسائر المجردات ليست\nعبارة عن مبدأ الحس والحركة كما في الحيوان بما لا يخفى فيكون الاختصاص المستفاد من\nالنسبة حقيقيا فافهم","english":"On “Moreover, those who affirm them of God, etc.” The same applies to those who affirm them of immaterial beings generally. Life in the Necessary Existent and other immaterial beings, for example, does not mean the principle of sensation and motion that it means in animals. The specificity conveyed by the attribution would then be absolute. Understand.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.lower.05","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والاختلاف بينهما بالعارض الخ) أقول الأولى التعبير بما\nعبر به المصنف في شرح المقاصد حيث قال التمييز بينهما قد لا يكون إلا بعارض الخ\nانتهى وذلك لأن الاختلاف بينهما قد يكون بالذات أيضا كما إذا كان الحال نوعا من\nالكيفيات والملكة نوعا آخر فليتأمل","english":"On “Their difference is accidental, etc.” I say: the author’s expression in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid is preferable: “The distinction between them may be only through an accident,” and so forth. For their difference may also be intrinsic, as when the state is one species of quality and the habitus another. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.lower.06","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن الحال تصير ملكة الخ) وذلك كما أن","english":"On “A state becomes a habitus, etc.” This is like—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I219.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.upper.01","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"بذوات الأنفس الحيوانية ومعنى الاختصاص بها أن تلك الكيفيات توجد في\nالحيوان دون النبات والجماد فلا يرد أن بعض هذه كالحياة والعلم والقدرة والإرادة\nثابت للواجب فلا تكون مختصة بالحيوان على أن القائل بثبوتها له تعالى لا يقول\nباندراجها تحت الكيف ولا في سائر الأعراض ولا الجواهر أيضا","english":"[Psychic qualities are specific] to possessors of animal souls. “Specific” means that these qualities occur in animals, not plants or inanimate things. It is therefore no objection that some—life, knowledge, power and will—belong to the Necessary Existent and are thus not specific to animals. Moreover, those who affirm them of God do not class them as qualities, other accidents, or substances.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I217.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I218.upper.02","volume":1,"page":218,"physical_pdf_page":231,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وسمي مع\nالرسوخ) في موضوعها واستكمالها فيه بحيث لا تزول عنه أصلا أو يعسر زوالها\n(ملكة وبدونه حالا) والاختلاف بينهما بالعارض فإن الحال تصير ملكة\nبالتدريج (فمنها) أورد من التبعيضية إشارة إلى عدم الانحصار في الأنواع المذكورة\nوأما تخصيص المذكور بها فلكثرة مباحثها (الحياة) قدمها على سائر الأنواع","english":"“With firmness”—when established and perfected in its subject so that it does not disappear at all or disappears only with difficulty—“it is called a habitus; without it, a state.” Their difference is accidental, since a state gradually becomes a habitus. “Among them”: the partitive particle indicates that the types mentioned do not exhaust the class; these are singled out because discussions of them are numerous. “Life” is placed before the other types—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I219.upper.01","editorial":"Malaka is “habitus”, a stable acquired or established disposition; hal is “state”. The marginal critique of “accidental difference” follows separately.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I219.lower.01","volume":1,"page":219,"physical_pdf_page":232,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الشخص من الإنسان يكون صبيا ثم يكون شيخا ومثل ذلك وإن كان قد يسبق إلى\nالوهم بل يقع في عبارة القوم أنه هو ذلك الشخص بعينه لكنه ليس كذلك للقطع\nبتغاير العوارض الشخصية كذا ذكره المحققون","english":"[This is like] a human individual who is a child and then becomes an old man. Although one may initially imagine—and scholars sometimes write—that it is that very individual, it is not so [without qualification], because the individualising accidents are certainly different. So the investigators have stated.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I218.lower.06","editorial":"The gloss’s metaphysical claim is translated as stated. It is not silently replaced by a modern account of personal identity.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I219.lower.02","volume":1,"page":219,"physical_pdf_page":232,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله المراد من القوة هنا الخ)\nهذا جواب ذكره في شرح المقاصد من اعتراض أورد على دليل المغايرة وحاصل\nالاعتراض أن عدم الإحساس والحركة والتغذية لا يدل على عدم القوة لأن المراد\nبالقوة هنا مبدأ الأثر والتأثير سواء بالقوة أو بالفعل وحاصل الجواب هو أن مرادنا\nعبارة الحياة لقوة الحس والتغذية مغايرتها للقوة التي يصدر عنها ذلك بالفعل لا مغايرتها\nلها مطلقا فافهم","english":"On “Faculty here means, etc.” This answer is given in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid to an objection against the proof of difference. The objection is that absence of sensation, motion and nutrition does not establish absence of the faculty, since “faculty” here means the principle of the effect and efficacy, whether potential or actual. The reply is that our claim of life’s difference from the sensory and nutritive faculties concerns its difference from the faculty from which these actually issue, not an unrestricted difference. Understand.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"عبارة الحياة لقوة","reading":"مغايرة الحياة لقوة","status":"Probable restoration of a compressed printed word; English renders the argument as difference, not a verbal definition."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I219.lower.03","volume":1,"page":219,"physical_pdf_page":232,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فلا تكون هي هي الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد بعد ما صرح\nبهذا وليس معناه أن القوة اسم لما يصدر منه الأثر بالفعل فإنه ظاهر البطلان لما\nصرحوا أن قوة الحس والحركة في العضو المفلوج باقية لكنها عاجزة عن الإحساس\nوالحركة ثم يتوجه أن يقال لم لا يجوز أن يكون مبدأ جميع تلك الآثار قوة واحدة\nهي الحياة وقد تعرض للبعض دون البعض لخصوصية المانع انتهى","english":"On “It is therefore not identical with it, etc.” After stating this explicitly, Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says: this does not mean that “faculty” names what actually produces the effect. That would plainly be false, since they explicitly state that the faculty of sensation and motion remains in a paralysed organ but is unable to sense or move. The further objection is then possible: why may one faculty—life—be the principle of all these effects, some occurring and others not because of a particular impediment? End.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"وقد تعرض للبعض","reading":"وقد تعرض للبعض","status":"Printed clause is compressed. Compare whether a word concerning an impediment is missing before treating this as a final transcription."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I219.lower.04","volume":1,"page":219,"physical_pdf_page":232,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول فيه\nتأمل فإن المراد من قول المجيب فلا تكون هي هي هو أن الحياة ليست هي القوة\nالتي يصدر منها الحركة والإحساس والتغذية بالفعل لأنها ليست هي مطلق تلك القوة\nسواء بالقوة أو الفعل كما سبق في تحرير الاعتراض والجواب فحينئذ لا وجه للمنع","english":"I say: this requires consideration. The respondent’s “It is not identical with it” means that life is not the faculty from which motion, sensation and nutrition actually issue—not that it is not that faculty without restriction, whether potential or actual—as explained in formulating the objection and reply. There is then no basis for the denial—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I220.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"لأنها ليست هي مطلق تلك القوة","reading":"لا أنها ليست هي مطلق تلك القوة","status":"The negative contrast is the argument’s point; verify the spacing of لا أنها against the scan."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I219.upper.01","volume":1,"page":219,"physical_pdf_page":232,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لكونها أصلا ومستتبعة إياها (وهي قوة هي مبدأ القوة الحس والحركة)\nالإرادية ويدل على أنها غير قوة الحس والحركة وجودها في العضو المفلوج إذ هي\nالحافظة في الحيوان للأجزاء العنصرية المتداعية إلى الانفكاك عن التعفن\nوليس فيه قوة الحس والحركة وعلى أنها غير قوة التغذية وجودها في العضو الذابل\nمن غير اغتذاء والمراد من القوة ههنا ما يصدر عنه الأثر بالفعل بمعنى أنا نريد أن\nالقوة التي يصدر عنها الحس والحركة والتغذية بالفعل غير باقية فلا تكون هي هي","english":"[Life is placed first] because it is fundamental and entails the others. “It is a faculty that is the principle of the faculty of sensation and” voluntary “motion.” Its difference from the faculty of sensation and motion is indicated by its presence in a paralysed organ. In an animal, life preserves the elemental parts—which tend to separate—from putrefaction, although the organ lacks the faculty of sensation and motion. Its difference from the nutritive faculty is indicated by its presence in a withered organ without nutrition. “Faculty” here means that from which an effect actually issues. Our point is that the faculty actually producing sensation, motion and nutrition does not remain; life is therefore not identical with it.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"مبدأ القوة الحس","reading":"مبدأ قوة الحس","status":"The print’s definite article/construct wording is awkward; the intended faculty of sensation and motion is secure."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I220.lower.01","volume":1,"page":220,"physical_pdf_page":233,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"المذكور فحينئذ لم يتوجه إليه ولا معنى له فتأمل","english":"[There is no basis for] the denial mentioned: it does not address the reply and has no force against it. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I219.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I220.lower.02","volume":1,"page":220,"physical_pdf_page":233,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولذا قالوا بالاشتراط الخ)\nورد بأن غاية ما ذكر الدوران وهو لا يقتضي الاشتراط بحيث تنعدم الحياة بدون تلك\nالأمور واستدل بعض المتكلمين على امتناع كونه مشروطا بالبنية بأنها لو اشترطت\nفإما أن تقوم بجزئين من البنية حياة واحدة فيلزم قيام العرض بأكثر من محل واحد\nوقد مر بطلانه أو تقوم بكل جزء حياة وحينئذ إما أن يكون القيام بالكل مشروطا\nبالقيام بالآخر فيلزم الدور أولا فيلزم الرجحان بلا مرجح أو تقوم ببعض الأجزاء فقط\nفيلزم كون الحي هو ذلك البعض لا النفس المؤلفة هذا خلف وأجيب بأنها تقوم\nبالمجموع الذي هو البنية وليس هذا من قيام العرض بمحلين فيضي كما سبق\nفتدبر","english":"On “Which is why some affirm the conditions, etc.” The objection is that the most established is concomitance, which does not entail a condition such that life must be absent without these things. Some theologians argued that bodily constitution cannot be a condition: if it were, either one life inheres in two parts of the constitution, requiring an accident to inhere in more than one subject—already shown false—or a life inheres in each part. In the latter case, either inherence in each is conditioned by inherence in the other, producing a circle, or it is not, producing preference without a determining factor. Or life inheres only in some parts, in which case only those parts, not the composed whole itself, are alive: an absurdity. The reply is that life inheres in the composite which is the constitution. This is not an accident’s inherence in two subjects. [A short concluding word is unclear.] Consider this.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"بالكل مشروطا","reading":"بكل مشروطا","status":"The intended distributive structure is “in each”; verify the article against the scan."},{"locus":"فيضي كما سبق","reading":"فيضي كما سبق","status":"Unclear short word before كما سبق; no additional argumentative content invented."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I220.lower.03","volume":1,"page":220,"physical_pdf_page":233,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله عما اتصف بها الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد فيكون كالعمى الطارئ\nبعد البصر لا كمطلق العمى وحينئذ لا يكون عدم الحياة عن الجنين عند استعداده للحياة\nموتا انتهى أقول وفي هذا اعتبار لأمر آخر في عدم الملكة زائد على ما اعتبر فيه\nفيما سبق فافهم","english":"On “In what was qualified by life, etc.” Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says: it is like blindness occurring after sight, not blindness without restriction. The absence of life in an embryo when it is prepared for life would therefore not be death. End. I say: this adds a further consideration to privation beyond what was previously included in it. Understand.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I220.lower.04","volume":1,"page":220,"physical_pdf_page":233,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وجودية الخ) وعليه يحمل قول بعض المعتزلة أن الموت فعل","english":"On “Positively existent, etc.” This is the interpretation of the statement of some Muʿtazila that death is an act—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I221.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I220.upper.01","volume":1,"page":220,"physical_pdf_page":233,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ولا تشترط) الحياة (باعتدال المزاج ولا بوجود البنية) وهي عند\nالحكماء جسم مؤلف من العناصر الأربعة له صورة نوعية وعند المعتزلة مبلغ من\nالجواهر الفردة التي يقوم بها تأليف خاص ولا تقوم الحياة بأقل منها","english":"Life “does not require a balanced temperament or the existence of a bodily constitution.” For the philosophers, a constitution is a body composed of the four elements and possessing a specific form; for the Muʿtazila, it is a quantity of indivisible substances in which a particular composition inheres, with life unable to subsist in fewer.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I220.upper.02","volume":1,"page":220,"physical_pdf_page":233,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ولا)\nبوجود (الروح) الحيواني قالوا وهو جسم لطيف حامل قوة الحياة إلى أعضاء\nالبدن يتولد من بخارية الأخلاط ينبعث من التجويف الأيسر من القلب ويسري\nإلى البدن من عروق نابتة من القلب تسمى بالشرايين وذلك للقطع بإمكان أن يخلقها\nالله تعالى في البسائط بل في الجزء الذي لا يتجزأ (وإن كانت قد تنتفي بفقدها) أي\nالمزاج والروح والبنية ولذا قالوا بالاشتراط","english":"“Nor” does it require the animal “spirit.” They say that this is a subtle body carrying the faculty of life to the body’s organs, generated from the vaporous component of the humours, issuing from the heart’s left cavity and spreading through the body in vessels growing from the heart, called arteries. [These are not necessary conditions] because God can certainly create life in simples, indeed in an indivisible part. “Although life may cease when they are absent”—temperament, spirit and constitution—which is why some affirm these conditions.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The anatomical and humoral account is the historical account in the source, not present-day medical guidance.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I220.upper.03","volume":1,"page":220,"physical_pdf_page":233,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والموت) عبارة عن (زوالها)\nعما اتصف بها فهو عدم ملكة لها (وقيل كيفية) وجودية (تضادها)\nلقوله تعالى خلق الموت والحياة والخلق لكونه بمعنى الإيجاد لا يتصور إلا فيما","english":"“Death” means “its cessation” in what was qualified by life; it is thus a privation of life. “It is also said to be a quality,” positively existent, “contrary to it,” on the basis of God’s saying that He “created death and life.” Since creation means bringing into existence, it is conceivable only in what—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I221.upper.01","editorial":"The Qurʾanic quotation is Q 67:2; the argument and its rebuttal are preserved separately.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.lower.01","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"من الله أو من الملك يقتضي زوال حياة جسم من غير جرح وكأنهم احترزوا بالقيد\nالأخير من القتل لكن حمل الفعل على الكيفية المضادة للحياة مبني على أن المراد به الأثر\nالصادر من الفاعل إذ لو أريد به حقيقة الفعل والتأثير لكان تفسير الإماتة لا الموت\nكذا قالوا","english":"[It is an act] from God or from the angel, requiring the removal of a body’s life without a wound. The last qualification apparently excludes killing. But interpreting “act” as the quality contrary to life assumes that it means the effect issuing from the agent. If it meant the reality of acting and producing an effect, it would define causing death, not death itself. So they have said.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I220.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.lower.02","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهو يتعلق بالوجود والعدم الخ) وقد سبق مرارا أن العدم الخاص\nيصح أن يكون أثرا فتدبر","english":"On “It relates to existence and nonexistence, etc.” It has repeatedly been explained that a specific nonexistence can be an effect. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.lower.03","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كثير في الكلام الخ) وهو وإن كان خلاف الظاهر\nكاف في دفع الاحتجاج","english":"On “Frequent in speech, etc.” Although contrary to the apparent meaning, it suffices to defeat the argument.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.lower.04","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وقد يطلق) أي حقيقة أو مجازا على عدم الحياة من غير اعتبار\nكون الموضوع قابلا لها فلا يكون التقابل بينهما من تقابل العدم والملكة","english":"On “May also be applied.” Literally or figuratively, it is applied to the absence of life without considering whether the subject is capable of life. Their opposition is therefore not privation and possession.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.lower.05","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بأن\nالحضور عند المدرك الخ) يعني لا حاجة إلى جعل الإدراك مصدرا لمجهول كما أشار\nإليه في شرح المقاصد","english":"On “Presence to the apprehender, etc.” That is, there is no need to make “apprehension” a verbal noun in a passive sense, as indicated in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.lower.06","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إلا على القول بالشبح الخ) جمهور الحكماء في بيان العلم","english":"On “Except on the representational-likeness account, etc.” In explaining knowledge, the majority of the philosophers—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I222.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله على القول بالشبح) فإن الإدراك على هذا هو من الكيفيات الخارجية العارضة\nللنفس اه منه","english":"On “On the representational-likeness account.” Apprehension on this account is one of the extramental qualities occurring to the soul. End. From him.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.ruled.02","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إما بحقيقته) إشارة إلى أن قيد الحيثية ليس داخلا حتى يجعله\nاعتباريا بل المراد منه أن تلك الصورة من هذا النحو من الوجود علم وأنه من قبيل\nالكيفية حينئذ اه منه","english":"On “Either in its reality.” This indicates that the qualification of respect is not included so as to make it a matter of consideration. Rather, what is meant is that the form, in this mode of existence, is knowledge; it then belongs to the category of quality. End. From him.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.upper.01","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"له وجود ورد بأنه بمعنى التقدير وهو يتعلق بالموجود والمعدوم أيضا ولو سلم\nفالمراد بخلق الموت إحداث أسبابه على حذف المضاف ومثل ذلك كثير في الكلام\n(وقد يطلق) الموت (على عدمها كالجماد) فالتقابل بينهما حينئذ تقابل\nالإيجاب والسلب","english":"[The argument concludes that death] has existence. It is answered that “creation” means determination, which relates to existent and nonexistent alike. Even if that is conceded, “creating death” means bringing about its causes, with the governing noun omitted; such usage is frequent in speech. Death “may also be applied to the absence” of life, “as in an inanimate thing.” In that case the opposition is affirmation and negation.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I220.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.upper.02","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومنها الإدراك وهو تميز وحضور وظهور للشيء عند العقل)\nاعترض عليه بأن الإدراك صفة المدرك والتميز ونحوه صفة المدرك وأجاب عنه\nالمصنف بأن الحضور عند المدرك صفة ثم لا يخفى أنه بهذا التفسير إضافة لا كيف\nإلا أن يحمل على المسامحة ويكون الإدراك هو الصورة الحاضرة والمتميزة عنده من\nحيث هي كذلك لكنه حينئذ يكون من الأمور الاعتبارية فلا يكون من الموجودات\nفضلا عن كونه من أقسام الكيف إلا على القول بالشبح فافهم","english":"“Among them is apprehension: a thing’s being distinguished, present and manifest to the intellect.” The objection is that apprehension is an attribute of the apprehender, whereas being distinguished and the like are attributes of the apprehended. The author replies that presence to the apprehender is an attribute [of the apprehender]. Nevertheless, on this explanation apprehension is a relation, not a quality—unless the wording is taken loosely and apprehension means the form present and distinguished in the apprehender insofar as it is such. It would then be a matter of consideration, not an existent, let alone a division of quality, except on the representational-likeness account. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The unvocalised printed المدرك has two different roles here: apprehender (mudrik) and apprehended (mudrak). Shabaḥ is rendered “representational likeness”, not a ghost or a bodily image.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I221.upper.03","volume":1,"page":221,"physical_pdf_page":234,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"إما (بحقيقته)","english":"[The thing is present] either “in its reality”—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I223.upper.01","editorial":"There is no intervening principal text on I:222: that entire page continues the lower commentary.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I222.lower.01","volume":1,"page":222,"physical_pdf_page":235,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"افترقوا فرقتين فرقة يقال لهم أهل الحقيقة والأخرى أهل الشبح وبيانه كما ذكروه\nهو أنا إذا أدركنا شيئا فلا خفاء في أنه يحصل لنا حال لم يكن لنا قبل الإدراك\nوبكذا تشهد الفطرة بأنه بحصول أمر لم يكن لا بزوال أمر كان فإنا قد ندرك ما لا\nوجود له في الخارج وكثيرا ما يوجد شيء في الخارج ولا ندركه فتلك الحال بوجود\nذلك الشيء عند العقل بمعنى أنه يحصل له أثر مناسب ذلك الشيء ومميز عن غيره فمنهم\nمن قال مناسبة ذلك الأثر لذلك الشيء هو أنه بحيث لو وجد في الخارج لكان نفس\nذلك الشيء وهم أهل الحقيقة القائلون بأن العلم عين المعلوم ومنهم من قال تلك المناسبة هي\nأنه بحيث لو وجد فيه كان كيفية وشبحا منطبقا على ذلك الشيء لا عين ذلك الشيء كالصورة\nالمنقوشة من الفرس على الجدار مثلا فإنها ليست نفس الفرس بل شبحه المساوي\nوالمنطبق عليه وهم أهل الشبح القائلون بأن العلم غير المعلوم بل شبحه","english":"[The philosophers] divided into two groups: one called the proponents of reality, and the other the proponents of the representational likeness. Their explanation is this. When we apprehend something, we plainly acquire a state we did not have before apprehension. Natural awareness testifies that this occurs through the acquisition of something previously absent, not the disappearance of something previously present. We may apprehend what has no external existence; conversely, something may exist outside us without our apprehending it. The state therefore consists in the thing’s existence in the intellect, in the sense that an effect corresponding to that thing and distinguishing it from others occurs there. Some hold that the effect’s correspondence means that, if externally existent, it would be the very thing itself. They are the proponents of the reality, who hold that knowledge is identical with the known. Others hold that, if externally existent, it would be a quality, a likeness matching that thing rather than the thing itself. A picture of a horse inscribed on a wall, for example, is not the horse itself but a corresponding likeness matching it. They are the proponents of the representational likeness, who hold that knowledge differs from the known and is its likeness.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I221.lower.06","editorial":"This entire printed page is lower-register commentary. Its discussion is not assigned to the principal sharḥ merely because it occupies the page.","editorial_review_notes":["2026-11-12: restored printed فرقة after فرقتين; verified against original scan I:222. Earlier typed Arabic had dropped this noun."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I222.lower.02","volume":1,"page":222,"physical_pdf_page":235,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وبهذا ظهر\nأن الشبح الحق من أقسام الكيف لكن يرد أنه لا يعد من العلم إلا من حيث حضوره\nعند العقل كالصورة الحاضرة التي هي حقيقة الشيء بلا فرق فإن أخرج قيد الحيثية\nالمذكورة الصورة عن أقسام الموجودات أخرج الشبح أيضا وإلا فلا نعم على تقدير\nعدم الإخراج يكون مطلق الشبح كيفا بخلاف الصورة الحقيقية فإنها قد تكون وقد\nلا تكون","english":"This shows that the real representational likeness belongs to the divisions of quality. An objection remains: it is knowledge only insofar as it is present to the intellect, just like the present form which is the thing’s reality. There is no difference. If that qualification of respect excludes the form from the divisions of existents, it excludes the likeness as well; if not, it excludes neither. Granted, on the supposition that it does not exclude them, every representational likeness is a quality, whereas the form of the reality may or may not be a quality.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[],"proof_notes":["لا يعد من العلم confirmed in enlarged original; the English already expressed the restriction."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I222.lower.03","volume":1,"page":222,"physical_pdf_page":235,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ثم اعلم أنه قد ذكر في شرح المقاصد أن نسبة الحصول إلى الصورة في العقل\nكنسبة الوجود إلى الماهية في الخارج فكما ليس للماهية تحقق في الخارج ولعارضها\nالمسمى بالوجود تحقق آخر كذا ليس للصورة تحقق في العقل ولعارضها المسمى بالحصول\nتحقق آخر وبهذا الاعتبار يصح جعل العلم تارة نفس الصورة وتارة انتهى وحينئذ فلا\nإشكال في العبارة أصلا فافهم قال في الحاشية إشارة إلى أن قيد الحيثية إنما يجعل المقيد من الأمور الاعتبارية لو جعل جزءا بأن يجعل العلم عبارة عن المقيد والقيد وأما\nإذا جعل خارجا فلا بأن يكون العلم عبارة عن نفس الصورة كما أن المعلوم كذلك إلا\nأن العلم هو الصورة إذا حصلت عند العاقلة بخلاف المعلوم فإنه لم يختص بذلك ولذا\nاقتصر اتحادهما مع ما في الخارج انتهى","english":"Know also that Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid states that the relation of occurrence to the form in the intellect is like the relation of existence to quiddity outside it. Just as the quiddity does not have one external actuality and its concomitant called existence another, the form does not have one actuality in the intellect and its concomitant called occurrence another. In this consideration knowledge may properly be made sometimes the form itself and sometimes [its occurrence—*ḥuṣūluhā*, supplied conjecturally from the parallel, not printed here]. End. There is then no difficulty at all in the expression; understand. It is said in the gloss: this indicates that the qualification of respect would make the qualified thing a matter of consideration only if made part of it, so that knowledge comprised both the qualified thing and the qualification. Not so if the qualification is external: knowledge would be the form itself, as is the known, except that knowledge is the form when it occurs in the intellective faculty, whereas the known is not restricted to that. Hence their identity with what is external was restricted [in the manner just described]. End.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"وتارة انتهى","reading":"وتارة [الحصول؟] انتهى","status":"The second predicate is not visible in this witness; the English marks the unfinished phrase rather than treating a contextual supply as source."},{"locus":"اقتصر اتحادهما","reading":"اقتصر اتحادهما","status":"Enlargement confirms the visible wording. Its precise restriction remains a contextual interpretation, marked in brackets."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false,"critical_apparatus":[{"kind":"printed_lacuna_conjectural_supply","printed_arabic":"فيصح جعل العلم تارة نفس الصورة وتارة انتهى","parallel_arabic":"فبهذا الاعتبار يصح جعل العلم تارة نفس الصورة وتارة حصولها","suggested_supply":"[حصولها]","citation":"Taftāzānī, Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid, vol. I, p.227, digital source in cited research register","interpretation":"In the source quotation the contrast is the mental form itself vs its obtaining. The base print of Taqrīb appears to omit حصولها. The parallel is not an independent Taqrīb copy.","source_not_changed":true}]},{"id":"I222.lower.04","volume":1,"page":222,"physical_pdf_page":235,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول المعلوم إن كان عبارة عما في الخارج لا يعد\nمعلوما إلا إذا اعتبر من حيث صورته عند العاقلة غاية الأمر أن الحيثية خارجة كما أن\nالعلم فلا فرق فتدبر","english":"I say: if “the known” means the external thing, it is counted as known only when considered in respect of its form in the intellective faculty. At most, that qualification of respect is external, just as it is in the case of knowledge. There is therefore no difference. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I223.lower.01","volume":1,"page":223,"physical_pdf_page":236,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويقال له العلم الحضوري) قالوا الشيء المدرك إما أن لا يكون خارجا عن\nالمدرك كالنفس وصفاتها فتكون حقيقته المتعقلة عند المدرك نفس حقيقته الموجودة في الخارج\nولا يكون ذلك إدراكا دائما وإما أن يكون خارجا وحينئذ إما أن يكون موجودا فتكون حقيقته\nالمتعقلة صورة منتزعة من ذلك أولا بأن يكون معدوما ولا يلزم أن يكون دائما ويسمى إدراك\nالأول حضوريا وما عداه حصوليا","english":"On “It is called knowledge by presence.” They say: the apprehended thing either is not external to the apprehender, as with the soul and its attributes, in which case its intelligible reality in the apprehender is the very reality existing externally—and this does not mean that apprehension is perpetual—or it is external. In the latter case it may exist, so that its intelligible reality is a form abstracted from it, or not, being nonexistent. Nor does apprehension have to be perpetual. Apprehension of the first kind is called knowledge by presence, and the others knowledge by acquisition.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"ولا يكون ذلك إدراكا دائما","reading":"ولا يكون ذلك إدراكا دائما","status":"Visible wording denies perpetual apprehension; do not silently convert it to an affirmative standard account."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I223.lower.02","volume":1,"page":223,"physical_pdf_page":236,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله واعترض بأن الحضور الخ) واعترض من وجهين\nآخرين أيضا أحدهما أن يكون إدراك النفس لذاتها وصفاتها دائما لدوام الحضور\nواللازم باطل لأن كثيرا من صفاتها مما لا تطلع عليه إلا بعد التأمل والنظر وأجيب\nبمنع مقدمات بطلان اللازم فافهم وثانيهما أن النفس إذا كانت عالمة بذاتها وصفاتها\nكانت عالمة بعلمها بذلك وهكذا فيتسلسل العلوم وأجيب بأنها عبارات عقلية إذ\nليس هناك إلا شيء واحد يتعدد الاعتبار ويرجع إلى أن حصول الشيء وحضوره\nلا يزيد عليه في الواقع كوجوده","english":"On “The objection is that presence, etc.” Two further objections were raised. First, the soul’s apprehension of itself and its attributes would be perpetual, since presence is perpetual. That is false: many of its attributes are disclosed to it only after reflection and inquiry. The reply rejects the premises for the alleged falsity of this consequence. Understand. Second, if the soul knows itself and its attributes, it knows its knowledge of them, and so on; cognitions regress without end. The reply is that these are intellectual expressions: there is only one thing, considered in different ways. The point is that a thing’s occurrence and presence add nothing to it in reality, just as its existence does not.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I223.lower.03","volume":1,"page":223,"physical_pdf_page":236,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله المساوية الخ) اعترض الإمام بأن العلم لو كان\nبحصول الصورة المادية التي تسمى ماهية لزم من تصور الحرارة كون المدركة\nحارة وكذا في جميع الكيفيات وهو مع ظهور فساده يستلزم اجتماع الضدين\nعند تصورهما","english":"On “Equal, etc.” The Imām objected: if knowledge consisted in the occurrence of the material form called quiddity, conceiving heat would make the apprehending faculty hot, and likewise for every quality. Besides its evident falsity, this would require contraries to coexist when both are conceived.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I223.lower.04","volume":1,"page":223,"physical_pdf_page":236,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مع كونها مغايرة للهوية الخ) إشارة إلى الجواب عن الاعتراض","english":"On “Although different from the individual reality, etc.” This indicates the reply to the objection—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I224.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I223.upper.01","volume":1,"page":223,"physical_pdf_page":236,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"المحققة في الخارج ويقال له العلم الحضوري لأن المدرك حاضر بنفسه\nلا بصورته عند المدرك (كما في العلم بالنفس وصفاتها) فإن النفس مدركة\nلها ولصفاتها بلا واسطة صورة مغايرة لصورتهما الخارجية واعترض بأن الحضور\nيقتضي شيئين ضرورة فيمتنع علم الشيء بنفسه وأجيب بأن النفس من حيث\nإنها مشاهدة غيرها من حيث إنها مشهودة وهذا القدر كاف","english":"[It is present in its reality] actualised externally. This is called knowledge by presence, because the apprehended is itself present to the apprehender, not represented there by its form, “as in knowledge of the soul and its attributes.” The soul apprehends itself and its attributes without the mediation of a form different from their external form. The objection is that presence necessarily requires two things, making a thing’s knowing itself impossible. The reply is that the soul as witnessing differs from the soul as witnessed, and this degree of distinction suffices.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I221.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I223.upper.02","volume":1,"page":223,"physical_pdf_page":236,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(أو بصورته المنتزعة)\nمنه المساوية له المرتسمة في العقل أو آلته (كما في الماديات أو بصورته الحاصلة)\nفي العقل (ابتداء) من غير افتقار إلى الانتزاع (كما في المجردات والمعدومات)\n(وهي) أي الصورة (مع كونها مغايرة للهوية) الخارجية (التي بها الاتصاف)","english":"“Or through its form abstracted” from it, equal to it and impressed in the intellect or its instrument, “as in material things; or through its form obtained” in the intellect “from the outset,” without needing abstraction, “as in immaterial and nonexistent things.” “It”—the form—“although different from the” external “individual reality through which qualification occurs”—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I224.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I224.lower.01","volume":1,"page":224,"physical_pdf_page":237,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"المذكور آنفا بأن الحار ما قام به هوية الحرارة التي هي جزئية متلبسة بالعوارض\nفاعلة للآثار الخارجية لا صورتها وماهيتها الكلية التي هي مجردة عن ترتب الآثار","english":"[The reply to the objection] just mentioned is that a hot thing is one in which the individual reality of heat inheres: it is particular, invested with incidental features and productive of external effects. It is not one in which the form or universal quiddity inheres, since that is free of those effects.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I223.lower.04","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I224.lower.02","volume":1,"page":224,"physical_pdf_page":237,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ومعنى مساواتها الخ) أي ما ذكر من المغايرة لا ينافي مساواتها الهوية فإنها\nبمعنى أنها بحيث لو وجدت الخ","english":"On “Equality means, etc.” The stated difference does not conflict with the form’s equality to the individual reality, because equality means that, if it existed [externally, it would be that thing].","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I224.lower.03","volume":1,"page":224,"physical_pdf_page":237,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إنما هو في حصول العرض الخ) هذا جواب\nآخر من اعتراض الإمام وحاصله أن حصول الشيء يقال لمعان متعددة كحصول\nالمال لصاحبه والسواد للجسم والصورة للمدركة والصورة للمادة وكل منهما للجسم\nوالحاضر لما يحضر عنده وبالعكس ولزوم الاتصاف إنما هو في حصول العرض لمحله\nدون البواقي وليس الحصول الإدراكي كذلك فلا يستلزم الاتصاف ثم لما ظهر\nأن الحصول الإدراكي لا يستلزم الحصول الاتصافي علم أنه لا استلزام في عكسه أيضا\nحيث لا يستلزم الاتصاف الإدراك أيضا كما أشار إليه بقوله يتصف بالكرم\nولا يتصوره","english":"On “Only an accident’s occurrence, etc.” This is another reply to the Imām’s objection. “A thing’s occurrence” is used in several senses: wealth belonging to its owner, blackness to a body, a form to the apprehending faculty, form to matter, each of the latter to the body, the present thing to that to which it is present, and conversely. Qualification necessarily follows only an accident’s occurrence in its subject, not the others. Apprehensional occurrence is not of that kind and does not entail qualification. Once this is evident, the absence of an implication in the converse direction is also known: qualification likewise does not entail apprehension, as indicated by “He is qualified by generosity without conceiving it.”","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I224.lower.04","volume":1,"page":224,"physical_pdf_page":237,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ويندفع أيضا ما ذكر الإمام من أن الإدراك إذا كان نفس الحصول\nفالمدرك هو الذي له حصول الشيء فكان الجسم الحار مدركا للحرارة وأقول يندفع\nبه أيضا ما سبق من لزوم دوام علم النفس بصفاتها فتدبر","english":"This also answers the Imām’s point that, if apprehension is occurrence itself, the apprehender is what has the thing occurring to it, so a hot body would apprehend heat. I say: it also answers the earlier alleged consequence that the soul must perpetually know its attributes. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I224.lower.05","volume":1,"page":224,"physical_pdf_page":237,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله قلنا الصورة قد\nتؤخذ الخ) هذا السؤال والجواب مذكوران في شرح المقاصد بعد ذكر الاعتراض","english":"On “We reply: the form may be taken, etc.” This question and answer occur in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid after the objection—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I225.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I224.upper.01","volume":1,"page":224,"physical_pdf_page":237,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فإن الهوية فاعلة الصفات الخارجية وعليها تترتب الآثار بخلاف الصورة ومعنى\nمساواتها لها أنها بحيث إذا وجدت في الخارج كانت إياها (ليس حصولها\nفي الذهن كحصول العرض في المحل فلا يوجب) حصولها فيه مع مساواتها\nللخارجية (اتصاف المدرك بالمدرك) لأن لزوم الاتصاف إنما هو في حصول\nالعرض لمحله ووجود الصورة في الذهن ليس كذلك بل هو عبارة عن حضورها\nوظهورها عنده (فالكريم يتصور البخل ولا يتصف به ويتصف بالكرم ولا\nيتصوره) فإن قيل إذا لم يكن حصولها فيه حصولا اتصافيا واتصاف الذهن\nبالعلم ضروري فكيف يكون العلم عبارة عنها قلنا الصورة قد تؤخذ من حيث","english":"For that individual reality produces the external attributes and is the basis of their effects, unlike the form. Equality means that, if the form existed externally, it would be that very thing. “Its occurrence in the mind is not like an accident’s occurrence in its subject; therefore it does not entail”—despite its equality with the external thing—“the apprehender’s being qualified by the apprehended.” Qualification follows an accident’s occurrence in its subject. A form’s existence in the mind is not of that kind: it means its presence and manifestation to the mind. “The generous person conceives miserliness without being qualified by it, and is qualified by generosity without conceiving it.” If it is objected that, since the form does not occur in the mind by way of qualification, yet the mind’s qualification by knowledge is necessary, how can knowledge mean the form?—we reply: the form may be taken in respect of—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I223.upper.02","join_out":"I225.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I225.lower.01","volume":1,"page":225,"physical_pdf_page":238,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"والجواب المشهور بين الأنام وحاصل ذلك الاعتراض هو أن الصورة العلمية\nعرض قائم بالنفس حاصل فيها حصول الاتصاف وجعلوها نفس ماهية المعلوم والمطابقة\nللموجود العيني الذي ربما يكون من الجواهر فيلزم كونها عرضا وجوهرا وأيضا\nجعلوها كلية مع أن القائم بالنفس جزئي ضرورة فيلزم كونها كلية وجزئية وحاصل\nجوابه هو أن الممتنع كون الشيء الواحد من وجه واحد جوهرا وعرضا وكليا وجزئيا\nوأما عند اختلاف الوجوه فلا وكون تلك الصورة عرضا إنما هو من جهة قيامها\nبالموضوع الذي هو النفس وكونها جوهرا من حيث إنها ماهية إذا وجدت في الخارج\nكانت لا في الموضوع وكذا كونها جزئية من حيث قيامها بالنفس الجزئية وكونها كلية\nمن حيث مطابقتها الأفراد بمعنى أن الحاصل في العقل من كل منها عند التجرد عن\nالعوارض يكون تلك الصورة بعينها فلا تناقض انتهى","english":"[This is] the answer widely circulated among people. The objection amounts to this: the cognitive form is an accident inhering in the soul, occurring in it by way of qualification. Yet they make it the very quiddity of the object known, corresponding to an extramental existent that may be a substance. It must therefore be both accident and substance. They also make it universal, although what inheres in the soul is necessarily particular; it must therefore be both universal and particular. The answer is that what is impossible is for one thing, in one respect, to be substance and accident, universal and particular. There is no impossibility when the respects differ. The form is an accident insofar as it inheres in a subject, namely the soul; it is a substance insofar as it is a quiddity which, if existent externally, would not exist in a subject. Likewise, it is particular insofar as it inheres in a particular soul, and universal insofar as it corresponds to the individuals: what is obtained in the intellect from each, when stripped of incidental features, would be that very form. There is therefore no contradiction. End.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I224.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I225.lower.02","volume":1,"page":225,"physical_pdf_page":238,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أقول هذا غاية ما ذكروه\nفي تحقيق مسألة العلم وبعد لا يخلو عن الإشكال وذلك أنهم إن أرادوا بالوجوه\nالمختلفة ههنا الوجوه الاعتبارية يلزم أن يكون الجوهر والعرض من الأمور الاعتبارية\nمع أنهما من الأمور الحقيقية وإن أرادوا بها الوجوه الحقيقية كأن أرادوا أن تلك الصورة\nفي وجودها الذهني عرض وفي وجودها الخارجي جوهر ورد أنهما حينئذ متغايران بالحقيقة\nلا بالاعتبار فيمتنع القول باتحادهما فلا يتم أن العلم نفس المعلوم","english":"I say: this is the furthest their investigation of knowledge has gone, yet difficulties remain. If by the different respects here they mean differences merely of consideration, substance and accident must be matters of consideration, whereas they are real matters. If they mean real differences—for example, that the form is an accident in its mental existence and a substance in its external existence—the objection is that the two then differ really, not merely in consideration. They cannot be said to be identical, and it is not established that knowledge is the object known itself.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I225.lower.03","volume":1,"page":225,"physical_pdf_page":238,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وأيضا لو كان الجوهر عبارة عن\nماهية لو وجدت في الخارج كانت لا في موضوع لصدق على المعدوم الذي لو وجد لاستغنى\nعن الموضوع والعرض هو الموجود المفتقر إليه فحينئذ لا يتم القول بأن الصورة\nالعلمية القائمة بالنفس جوهر كما لا يخفى فالتحقيق أن مماثلة العلم للمعلوم هي المساواة\nالشائعة التي لا تنافي المغايرة الحقيقية فتدبر فإنه من المزالق لكن لنا تحقيق يندفع به\nجميع الشبهات التي تورد في هذا المقام فاطلبه في رسالتنا المعمولة في الحكمة الجديدة","english":"Moreover, if substance means a quiddity which, if externally existent, would not exist in a subject, the description applies to a nonexistent thing which, if existent, would dispense with a subject. An accident, however, is an existent dependent on a subject. It is then not established that the cognitive form inhering in the soul is a substance. The sound account is that the likeness of knowledge to the known is the familiar equality that does not exclude a real difference. Consider this carefully: it is a place where one may slip. We, however, have an investigation that removes all the difficulties raised here; seek it in our treatise composed on the new philosophy.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The first-person reference to “our treatise ... on the new philosophy” belongs to this unassigned marginal voice. It is evidence for future attribution, not permission to assign the gloss to Sanandaji or Wasim.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I225.lower.04","volume":1,"page":225,"physical_pdf_page":238,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وفيه ما مر الخ) كتب في الحاشية من أنه حينئذ يكون من الاعتبارات فإن المأخوذ من\nحيث الحصول كذلك انتهى وقد سبق هناك منا ما ينبغي مراجعته","english":"On “The earlier difficulty applies here.” It is written in the gloss that it would then belong to matters of consideration, because what is taken in respect of occurrence is of that kind. End. Our earlier discussion there should be consulted.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I225.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":225,"physical_pdf_page":238,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"*(١٥ - تقريب أول)*","english":"Printer’s gathering signature: “15 — Taqrīb, first [volume].”","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"Printed below the ruled text; not part of the philosophical argument.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I225.upper.01","volume":1,"page":225,"physical_pdf_page":238,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الحصول في النفس فتكون عرضا قائما بها حاصلا لها حصولا متأصلا اتصافيا\nفيكون موجودا عينيا كسائر صفاتها وفيه ما مر وقد تؤخذ من حيث ذاتها مع","english":"[It may be considered in respect of] its occurrence in the soul. It is then an accident inhering in the soul, belonging to it through a real occurrence by way of qualification, and is therefore an extramentally existent thing like its other attributes. The earlier difficulty applies here. It may also be considered in itself, while—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I224.upper.01","join_out":"I226.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I226.lower.01","volume":1,"page":226,"physical_pdf_page":239,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فتكون صورة للموجود العيني الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد وهي بهذا الاعتبار مفهوم\nلا تحقق له إلا في الذهن وإطلاق المعلوم عليها تجوز لأن المعلوم ما له صورة في العقل\nلا نفس الصورة انتهى أقول هذا ما عليه الجمهور من أن المعلوم في الموجودات هو\nالأمر العيني وأما من يقول إن المعلوم مطلقا سواء في الموجود والمعدوم هو الصورة\nالنفسية من حيث ذاتها كما يأتي فإطلاق المعلوم عنده على الموجود العيني تجوز عكس\nما عليه الجمهور","english":"On “It is then a form of the extramental existent, etc.” Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says: under this consideration it is a concept having actuality only in the mind; calling it “the known” is figurative, because the known is what has a form in the intellect, not the form itself. End. I say: this is the majority position, according to which the known, in cases involving existents, is the extramental thing. But for one who holds that the known without restriction—whether existent or nonexistent—is the psychic form considered in itself, as will follow, calling the extramental existent “the known” is figurative: the reverse of the majority position.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I226.lower.02","volume":1,"page":226,"physical_pdf_page":239,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لم تتصور الإضافة بينها وبين العالم الخ) وما قيل من\nإمكان تعلقها قائمة بأنفسها على ما هو رأي أفلاطون أو غيرها من الأجرام الثابتة مثلا","english":"On “No relation can be conceived between them and the knower, etc.” As for the suggestion that [forms] could be related to them while subsisting by themselves, on Plato’s view, or [subsisting] in other fixed bodies, for example—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I227.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"إمكان تعلقها قائمة بأنفسها","reading":"إمكان تعلقها قائمة بأنفسها","status":"Pronoun construction compressed; referents supplied in English brackets, not as extra Arabic."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I226.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":226,"physical_pdf_page":239,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولما كان الإشكال الخ) يعني أن الإشكال إنما يسلم لو كانت الإضافة مجردة\nعن الصورة التي بها يحصل الامتياز الذي يتوقف عليه الإضافة لكن القائلين بأن\nالعلم هو الإضافة لم يكونوا نافين الصورة وإنما ينفون كونها عين المعلوم وكون العلم\nعبارة عنها دون الإضافة التي بها الاتصاف دون الصورة كما لا يخفى اه منه","english":"On “Since the difficulty, etc.” That is: the difficulty is conceded only if the relation is stripped of the form through which the discrimination required by the relation is obtained. But those who say that knowledge is a relation did not deny the form. They deny that it is identical with the known and that knowledge means the form rather than the relation through which qualification occurs—not through the form. This is evident. End. From him.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The small ruled note qualifies the upper presentation; it must not be merged into Sanandaji’s own assertion. The personal identity behind منه is not established.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I226.upper.01","volume":1,"page":226,"physical_pdf_page":239,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"قطع النظر عن الحصول فتكون صورة للموجود العيني الذي ربما يكون من الجواهر\nفلا يتصف بها النفس","english":"[It may be considered in itself] while disregarding its occurrence. It is then a form of the extramental existent, which may be a substance; the soul is not qualified by it [under that consideration].","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I225.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I226.upper.02","volume":1,"page":226,"physical_pdf_page":239,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ومن أنكر الوجود العقلي جعل الإدراك مجرد إضافة)\nبين العالم والمعلوم بأن يكون العالم عالما والمعلوم معلوما وعليه فالتعريف المذكور\nعلى ظاهره وهذه الإضافة لابد منها في كون شيء عالما [جزما؟] ولم يثبت غيرها مما\nيعول عليه فلذا اقتصر عليها الجمهور والمتكلمون (أو) جعله (صفة ذات\nإضافة) والاعتراض بعلم الشيء بنفسه بأن التعلق لا يتصور إلا بين شيئين\nمدفوع بأن التغاير الاعتباري كاف كما مر","english":"“Those who deny mental existence make apprehension a mere relation” between knower and known, whereby the knower is a knower and the known is known. On this view, the stated definition retains its apparent meaning. Such a relation is indispensable for a thing’s being a knower [certainly]; and nothing else on which to rely has been established. This is why the majority and the theologians confined themselves to it. “Or” apprehension is made “an attribute possessing a relation.” The objection from a thing’s knowing itself—that a relation can be conceived only between two things—is answered by the sufficiency of a distinction in consideration, as explained earlier.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"after عالما","reading":"جزما","status":"Probable short word, retained as uncertain. The previous invented separation “حرروا أو” is superseded; ولم يثبت is secure."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I226.upper.03","volume":1,"page":226,"physical_pdf_page":239,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(فأشكل عليه العلم بالمعدومات)\nفإنه لا تحقق لها في الخارج فإذا لم يتحقق في الذهن أيضا لم تتصور الإضافة بينها\nوبين العالم بها (فلزم القول بالصورة في) العلم (بالمعدوم) حتى لا يرد ذلك\n(بل في الكل) فإنه إذا علم أنه غير الإضافة في فرد علم أنه كذلك مطلقا بناء على أنه\nأي الإدراك في الكل معنى واحد وحده قويم فلا يجوز اختلاف أفراده لكن\nالإشكال مندفع بأن الإضافة إنما تتوقف على الامتياز الذي لا يتوقف على وجود\nالمتمايزين كما أشرنا إليه في باب الأمور العامة ولما كان الإشكال بالمعدومات","english":"“Knowledge of nonexistent things is then a difficulty for him.” They have no external actuality; if they have no actuality in the mind either, no relation can be conceived between them and their knower. “It therefore becomes necessary to affirm a form in” knowledge “of the nonexistent,” to avoid that difficulty; “indeed, in every case.” If apprehension is found to be other than a relation in one instance, it must be so without restriction, on the assumption that apprehension has one meaning throughout and that its definition is sound: its instances cannot differ in this respect. The difficulty is answered, however, by saying that relation requires only discrimination, which does not require the two discriminated things to exist, as we indicated in the discussion of general matters. Since the difficulty concerning nonexistent things—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I227.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I227.lower.01","volume":1,"page":227,"physical_pdf_page":240,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"فضروري البطلان في الممتنعات على ما قالوا","english":"[That suggestion] is necessarily false in the case of impossible things, according to what they have said.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I226.lower.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I227.lower.02","volume":1,"page":227,"physical_pdf_page":240,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وتحقق ذلك المعدوم الخ) قال\nبعضهم وفي بعض المواضع من كلام ابن سينا اعتراف بأن العلم بالممتنعات ليس بحصول\nالصورة لأن المستحيل لا يحصل له صورة في العقل فلا يمكن أن يتصور شيء هو اجتماع\nالنقيضين مثلا بل تصوره إنما يكون على سبيل التشبيه بأن يعقل من السواد والحلاوة\nأمر هو الاجتماع ثم يقال هذا الأمر لا يكون السواد والبياض","english":"On “And that nonexistent became actual, etc.” Someone said: in some passages Ibn Sīnā acknowledges that knowledge of impossible things does not consist in the occurrence of a form. An impossible thing has no form in the intellect, so one cannot conceive a thing that is, for example, the conjunction of contradictories. Its conception occurs only by analogy: from blackness and sweetness one apprehends a feature, conjunction, and then says that this feature does not obtain for blackness and whiteness.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[],"proof_notes":["شيء هو اجتماع confirmed from enlarged original; the earlier حتى reading is superseded."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I227.lower.03","volume":1,"page":227,"physical_pdf_page":240,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والثابت باعتبار\nالصورة الخ) أي باعتبار ما مر أن حصول الصورة في العقل هو نفس الصورة ليس\nلكل منهما تحقق على حدة أو باعتبار الخبر","english":"On “Established in consideration of the form, etc.” That is, in light of the earlier point that the occurrence of a form in the intellect is the form itself, not something with a separate actuality of its own; or in consideration of the predicate.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I227.lower.04","volume":1,"page":227,"physical_pdf_page":240,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وقيل العلم والمعلوم) لا يخفى أن القول باتحاد\nالعلم والمعلوم أشد ملاءمة بهذا مما عليه الجمهور وتدبر","english":"On “Another view holds that knowledge and the known, etc.” It is evident that the identity of knowledge and the known fits this view more closely than the majority view. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I227.lower.05","volume":1,"page":227,"physical_pdf_page":240,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بلا شرط حضوره الخ)\nفيكون أعم من الإحساس إذ هو إدراك المادي مع الهيئة المذكورة سواء في حالة","english":"On “Without requiring its presence, etc.” It is therefore more general than sensation, since it is apprehension of the material thing with the stated configuration, whether in the state of—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I228.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I227.upper.01","volume":1,"page":227,"physical_pdf_page":240,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"واردا على القول بالصورة أيضا لأن الصورة إنما تكون لذي الصورة أشار إلى دفعه\nبقوله (ومعناها) أي الصورة للمعدوم (أن للمعدوم وجودا غير متأصل) بحيث\nلو أمكن تحققه في الخارج وتحقق ذلك المعدوم لكان إياه (وهي) أي الوجود الغير\nالمتأصل والثابت باعتبار الصورة (من حيث قيامها بالذهن) وحصولها فيه (علم)\nوصورة تصف الذهن (ومن حيث ذاتها) وماهيتها المتعقلة مع قطع النظر\nعن قيامها بالذهن (معلوم) وذو صورة (بخلاف الموجود فإن العلم في الذهن\nوالمعلوم في الخارج) وقيل العلم والمعلوم في الكل الصورة الذهنية فباعتبار\nقيامها بالعاقل علم وباعتبار ذاتها في نفسها معلوم","english":"[Since the difficulty] also applies to the form theory—a form is a form of something—the author indicates its answer: “The meaning of it,” a form of the nonexistent, “is that the nonexistent has a non-original existence,” such that, if its external actuality were possible and it became actual, it would be that very thing. “It”—the non-original existence established in consideration of the form—“insofar as it inheres in the mind” and occurs there, “is knowledge,” a form qualifying the mind. “In itself,” in its intelligible quiddity apart from inherence in the mind, it “is known,” that of which there is a form. “This differs from the existent: knowledge is in the mind, while the known is outside it.” Another view holds that, in every case, both knowledge and the known are the mental form: considered as inhering in the intellect it is knowledge; considered in itself it is known.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I226.upper.03","editorial":"“Non-original existence” renders وجود غير متأصل without treating the term as a fully developed theory independent of this context.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I227.upper.02","volume":1,"page":227,"physical_pdf_page":240,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وأنواع الإدراك) أربعة أحدها\n(إحساس) وهو إدراك الشيء الموجود في الخارج الحاصل عند المدرك على هيئة\nمخصوصة من الأين والوضع وغير ذلك (و) ثانيها (تخيل) وهو إدراكه\nمع الهيئة المذكورة لكن بلا شرط حضوره (و) ثالثها (توهم) وهو إدراك","english":"“The kinds of apprehension” are four. The first is “sensation”: apprehending something existing externally and present to the apprehender in a particular configuration of place, position and other features. The second is “imagination”: apprehending it with that configuration, but without requiring its presence. The third is “estimation”: apprehending—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I228.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.lower.01","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"حضوره أو غيبته","english":"[Whether in the state of] its presence or its absence.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I227.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.lower.02","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله غير محسوسة الخ) كالإضافات والكيفيات الغير المحسوسة","english":"On “Nonsensible, etc.” Such as relations and nonsensible qualities.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.lower.03","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من حيث هو هو الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد أي لا من حيث شيء آخر سواء\nأخذ وحده مطلقا أو مع غيره من الهيئات الحاصلة من حصوله في الذهن فقيد الحيثية\nلتقييد تجرده عن العوارض الخارجية فقوله آخر أي آخر خارجي فتدبره","english":"On “Insofar as it is itself, etc.” Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid says: not in respect of something else, whether taken by itself without restriction or with other configurations consequent upon its occurrence in the mind. The qualification of respect restricts its freedom [from concomitants] to external concomitants. “Something else” therefore means something else external. Consider this carefully.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.lower.04","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بالعوارض\nالذهنية الخ) مثل تشخصها من حيث حلولها في النفس الجزئية وعرضيتها من حيث\nقيامها بها ومقارنتها للصفات النفسية وفي كون هذه من العوارض الذهنية كلام\nسبق في مبحث الماهية","english":"On “Mental concomitants, etc.” For example, its individuation insofar as it lodges in a particular soul, its accidentality insofar as it inheres there, and its association with psychic attributes. Whether these count as mental concomitants was discussed earlier in the inquiry into quiddity.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.lower.05","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فالإبصار علم بالمبصرات الخ) ونقل من الجمهور ما حاصله\nأن الشيخ الأشعري إن أراد أن حقيقة إدراك الحس هي حقيقة إدراك المسمى\nالعلم بحيث لا تفاوت إلا في طريق الحصول كما في العلم الذي بطريق الاستدلال\nوالإلهام والحدس فممنوع لأنا نجد فرقا بينا بين العلم بهذا اللون وإبصاره والعلم\nبهذه الرائحة وشمها وهكذا وإن أراد أن العلم يطلق على نوع الإدراك بالحس فهو\nبحث لفظي راجع إلى التسمية انتهى وأقول الظاهر من عبارة المصنف في هذا\nالمتن هو أن المراد هو الثاني","english":"On “Vision is knowledge of visible objects, etc.” What is reported from the majority amounts to this. If Shaykh al-Ashʿarī means that the reality of sensory apprehension is the reality of the apprehension called knowledge, differing only in its means of acquisition—as with knowledge through inference, inspiration and intuition—we deny it. We find a clear difference between knowing this colour and seeing it, and between knowing this smell and smelling it, and likewise in other cases. If he means that “knowledge” is applied to the kind of apprehension occurring through the senses, the dispute is verbal, concerning nomenclature. End. I say: the author’s wording in this matn apparently intends the latter.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.lower.06","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لكن إطلاقه على الإحساس الخ) هذا إنما\nيضر القول بإطلاق العلم على الإدراك الحسي لو أريد به الإطلاق بحسب العرف\nأو اللغة أما لو أريد به الإطلاق باصطلاح الشيخ فلا فتفطن","english":"On “But applying it to sensation, etc.” This damages the claim that knowledge applies to sensory apprehension only if the claim concerns customary or linguistic usage. It does not do so if the intended usage is Shaykh al-Ashʿarī’s technical terminology. Take notice.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.lower.07","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مخالف الخ) فإنه\nفي العرف واللغة اسم لغير الإحساس من الإدراكات كما في شرح المقاصد","english":"On “Contrary, etc.” In customary and linguistic usage it names kinds of apprehension other than sensation, as stated in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.upper.01","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لمعان غير محسوسة مخصوصة بالشيء الجزئي الموجود في المادة (و) رابعها\n(تعقل) وهو إدراك الشيء من حيث هو هو فالإحساس مشروط بثلاثة أشياء\nحضور المادة واكتناف الهيئة وكون المدرك جزئيا والتخيل مجرد عن الشرط\nالأول والتوهم عن الأولين والتعقل عن الجميع بمعنى أن تكون الصورة مجردة\nعن العوارض المادية الخارجية وإن لم يمكن لها بد عن الاكتناف بالعوارض\nالذهنية","english":"[Estimation apprehends] nonsensible meanings specific to a particular thing existing in matter. The fourth is “intellection”: apprehension of a thing insofar as it is itself. Sensation requires three things: the presence of matter, accompaniment by the configuration, and the apprehended object’s being particular. Imagination dispenses with the first condition, estimation with the first two, and intellection with all of them. That means that the form is free of extramental material concomitants, although it cannot avoid being accompanied by mental concomitants.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I227.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I228.upper.02","volume":1,"page":228,"physical_pdf_page":241,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقد يقال العلم لمطلق الإدراك) الشامل للأربعة كما ذهب إليه الشيخ\nالأشعري حيث قال الإحساس بالشيء علم به فالإبصار علم بالمبصرات وهكذا لكن\nإطلاقه على الإحساس مخالف للعرف واللغة (و) قد يقال (للثلاثة الأخيرة)","english":"“Knowledge may be used for apprehension without restriction,” encompassing all four kinds. This was the view of Shaykh al-Ashʿarī, who said that sensing a thing is knowing it: vision is knowledge of visible objects. And so on. But applying the word to sensation is contrary to customary and linguistic usage. “It may also be used for the last three”—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I229.upper.01","readings":[],"proof_notes":["وهكذا confirmed in the enlarged original."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.lower.01","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله الغير الزائل الخ) اعترض على اعتبار الثبات في اليقين بأنه لو أريد به عسر\nالزوال فليكن اعتقاد المقلد كذلك وإن أريد به امتناع الزوال فاليقين الحاصل من النظر\nقد يذهل عن بعض مباديه فيشك فيه بل ربما يحكم بخلافه وأجيب بأنه لو أريد بالذهول\nمجرد حصول المبادئ بالفعل في العقل فإمكان طريان الشك حينئذ ممنوع وإن أريد\nبه الزوال إلى حيث يفتقر إلى تحصيل واكتساب فلا يقين حينئذ بذلك الحكم\nالنظري ونحن إنما نحكم بامتناع الشك في اليقين ما دام يقينا","english":"On “Not removed, etc.” Stability as a condition of certainty was challenged as follows. If it means difficulty of removal, a follower’s belief may be stable in that sense. If it means impossibility of removal, someone may lose awareness of some principles of a certainty acquired by inquiry, and then doubt it or even judge the opposite. The reply is: if “loss of awareness” means [the loss of] the principles’ mere actual presence in the intellect, the possibility of doubt arising at that point is disputed. If it means their removal to the point that they must be acquired afresh, there is then no certainty concerning that theoretical judgment. We affirm only that doubt is impossible in certainty so long as it remains certainty.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"مجرد حصول المبادئ بالفعل","reading":"مجرد حصول المبادئ بالفعل","status":"Visible print retained. An omitted negation/loss qualification is suspected from the objection; not silently emended. English interpretation is bracketed."}],"editorial":"The primary print visibly says مجرد حصول المبادئ بالفعل. The answer would normally contrast mere lack of actual attention with loss of retained principles. That interpretation is not a newly recovered variant.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.lower.02","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله اعتقادا) وقد\nلا يعتبر في الاعتقاد المطابقة فينقسم إلى الصحيح والفاسد فيكون أعم من الجهل\nالمركب لأنه مشروط بعدم المطابقة فهو قسم منه فقط","english":"On “Belief.” Correspondence may be omitted from the conditions of belief, which is then divided into sound and unsound belief. In that sense it is more general than compound ignorance, since compound ignorance requires lack of correspondence and is therefore only one subdivision of it.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.lower.03","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والجهل المركب والاعتقاد\nالخ) أي الاعتقاد بالمعنى المذكور في المتن أعني الصحيح كما قيده به في شرح المقاصد\nلا بالمعنى الأعم من الجهل المركب وإلا لزم جعل الأعم من الشيء قسيما له","english":"On “Compound ignorance and belief, etc.” That is, belief in the sense mentioned in the base text, namely sound belief, as he explicitly qualifies it in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid—not in the sense more general than compound ignorance. Otherwise what is more general than a thing would be made a coordinate division alongside that thing.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.lower.04","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لأن غير\nالجازم الخ) أي من التصديق كما ظهر","english":"On “For the non-decisive, etc.” That is, non-decisive assent, as has become clear.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.lower.05","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن قيل المراد بالشك الخ) وكذا يقال\nالمراد بالوهم الحكم بمرجوحية الطرف المرجوح","english":"On “If it is said that doubt means, etc.” Likewise, one may say that conjecture means judging the weaker alternative to be weaker.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.lower.06","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله قلنا هذا تصديق الخ)","english":"On “We reply: this is assent, etc.”—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I230.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.upper.01","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أي التخيل والتوهم والتعقل كما عليه الجمهور","english":"That is, imagination, estimative apprehension and intellection, as the majority maintain.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I228.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.upper.02","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) قد يقال (للأخير) أي\nالتعقل خاصة (وللتصديق) مطلقا فيعم اليقين وغيره وللتصديق\n(الجازم المطابق) للواقع (الثابت) الغير الزائل بتشكيك المشكك (فيسمى\nالخالي عن الجزم ظنا وعن المطابقة جهلا مركبا وعن الثبات اعتقادا) وقد يراد\nبالظن ما ليس بيقين فيعم الظن الصرف والجهل المركب واعتقاد المقلد وبالجملة\nينحصر التصديق في العلم والجهل المركب والاعتقاد والظن لأن غير الجازم لابد\nأن يكون راجحا لأنه أقل مراتب الحكم أعني إذعان الوقوع أو اللاوقوع","english":"“It” may also be applied “to the last,” intellection alone, “and to assent” without restriction, thus including certainty and other kinds; or to “decisive assent corresponding” to reality and “stable,” not removed by a doubter’s attempt to cast doubt. “What lacks decisiveness is called opinion; what lacks correspondence, compound ignorance; and what lacks stability, belief.” Sometimes “opinion” means whatever is not certainty, so it includes mere opinion, compound ignorance and the belief of one who follows authority. In short, assent is confined to knowledge, compound ignorance, belief and opinion. For non-decisive assent must favour one side: this is the lowest degree of judgment, namely acknowledging occurrence or non-occurrence.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The pronoun “it” concerns the term علم in the preceding classification. “Belief” here has the specified technical sense of assent lacking the stability of certainty, not every use of اعتقاد.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I229.upper.03","volume":1,"page":229,"physical_pdf_page":242,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وأما)\nما ذكره جمع من أن غير الجازم إن كان راجحا فظن أو مساويا فشك أو مرجوحا\nفوهم فلم يعرض به المصنف لأن (الشك) عنده عبارة عن نفس التردد (والوهم)\nملاحظة الطرف المرجوح (فـ) كلاهما (تصور بلا حكم) فإن قيل المراد\nبالشك الحكم بتساوي الطرفين عند العقل قلنا هذا تصديق ويكون أحد الأقسام","english":"“As for” what a group have said—that the non-decisive is opinion if preponderant, doubt if equal, and conjecture if outweighed—the author does not adopt that classification, since “doubt” for him is hesitation itself, while “conjecture” is attending to the outweighed alternative. “Both,” therefore, are “conception without judgment.” If it is said that doubt means judging the two alternatives equal in the intellect’s estimation, we reply: this is assent, and belongs to one of the divisions—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I230.upper.01","editorial":"Wahm here is the apprehension of the weaker alternative. It is not the internal estimative faculty denoted by wahm elsewhere.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.lower.01","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ولعل من عدهما من التصديق إنما أراد بهما هذا الذي ذكر فيكون النزاع لفظيا","english":"Perhaps those who counted both [doubt and conjecture] as assent intended precisely what has been described; the disagreement would then be verbal.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I229.lower.06","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.lower.02","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بمنزلة قولنا أنا شاك الخ) وقولك أنا متوهم الخ","english":"On “Just as our statement, ‘I am doubtful,’ etc.” And your statement, “I conjecture,” and so forth.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.lower.03","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وقد لا يفرق الخ)\nوعلى التقديرين يكون نسبتهما إلى العلم نسبة الموت إلى الحياة بمعنى أنهما عدم\nملكة العلم لكن مع خصوصية قيد الطريان كما مر","english":"On “Sometimes no distinction is drawn, etc.” On either account, their relation to knowledge is like the relation of death to life: they are privations of knowledge, but with the specific qualification that the privation supervenes, as previously explained.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.lower.04","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله عما من شأنه أن يكون الخ)\nفيكون الجهل البسيط أعم من السهو والنسيان فافهم","english":"On “In something of a nature to [know], etc.” Simple ignorance is therefore more general than inattention and forgetting. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.lower.05","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والجواب الخ) الظاهر\nأنه معارضة ويمكن تنزيله على المنع أيضا أي لا نسلم أن الاختلاف بالعرض\nلا يوجب الاختلاف بالذات إنما يكون كذلك لو لم يكن لازما من أخص الصفات","english":"On “The reply, etc.” Apparently this is a counterargument. It can also be construed as disputing a premise: we do not concede that difference in an accidental feature never entails intrinsic difference. That would hold only if the feature were not a concomitant belonging to the most specific characteristics.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.upper.01","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الأربعة بمنزلة قولنا أشك في هذا","english":"[It belongs to one of] the four divisions, just as does our statement, “I doubt this.”","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I229.upper.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.upper.02","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والذهول عن الصورة الإدراكية إن\nانتهى إلى الزوال فنسيان وإلا فسهو) وقد لا يفرق بين السهو والنسيان","english":"“Loss of awareness of an apprehended form is forgetting if it extends to the form’s disappearance; otherwise it is inattention.” Sometimes no distinction is drawn between inattention and forgetting.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"Sahw is rendered “inattention” here to distinguish it from disappearance of the retained form, not to impose one modern psychological taxonomy.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.upper.03","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والجهل)\nقسمان بسيط ومركب و(البسيط) عدم العلم عما من شأنه أن يكون عالما\nفهو (عدم ملكة العلم والمركب ضاده) لأنهما صفتان وجوديتان يستحيل\nاجتماعهما في محل واحد (وقيل مماثل له وامتناع اجتماعهما إنما هو للمماثلة\nلا للتضاد إذ الاختلاف) بينهما (بالإضافة) هو (الطباق) فإن العلم\nمطابق لمتعلقه والجهل غير مطابق له والطباق واللاطباق نسبتان والنسبة خارجة عن\nالمنتسبين عارضة لهما لكونها متأخرة عن طرفيها والجواب أن الطباق واللاطباق أخص\nصفاتهما فيلزم من الاختلاف فيهما الاختلاف في الذات","english":"“Ignorance” is of two kinds, simple and compound. “Simple” ignorance is the absence of knowledge in something of a nature to know; thus it “is a privation of knowledge, while compound ignorance is its contrary,” since knowledge and compound ignorance are two positive attributes that cannot coexist in one subject. “It has also been said that compound ignorance is of the same kind as knowledge, and that their inability to coexist is due to similarity, not contrariety, since the difference” between them “is relational”: it concerns “correspondence.” Knowledge corresponds to its object, ignorance does not. Correspondence and non-correspondence are two relations; a relation is external and accidental to its relata, since it is posterior to them. The reply is that correspondence and non-correspondence are their most specific characteristics; difference in these therefore entails difference in their intrinsic natures.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.upper.04","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم العلم إما قديم لا يسبقه\nالعدم وهو علم الله تعالى أو حادث يسبقه وهو علم الخلق (والعلم الحادث قد يكون\nبالقوة) المحضة (وهو الاستعداد) للعلم بالفعل وحصول الاستعداد للعلم\nالضروري يكون بالحواس كما يستفاد من مس النار أن هذه النار حارة\nفتستعد النفس للعلم بأن كل نار حارة وللعلم النظري يكون بحصول الضروريات","english":"Knowledge is either eternal, not preceded by nonexistence—God’s knowledge—or originated, preceded by nonexistence—the knowledge of creatures. “Originated knowledge may be” merely “potential, which is preparedness” for actual knowledge. Preparedness for necessary knowledge is acquired through the senses: touching fire, for example, yields awareness that this fire is hot, preparing the soul to know that every fire is hot. Preparedness for theoretical knowledge is acquired through necessary cognitions.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The example and the claim about universal fire are the author’s epistemological account, not a modern empirical claim asserted by the editor.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I230.upper.05","volume":1,"page":230,"physical_pdf_page":243,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقد يكون بالفعل إما إجمالا بأن يلاحظ أمر بسيط هو مبدأ التفاصيل) أي\nتفاصيل الأجزاء لمن علم مسألة فيسئل عنها فإنه يحضر الجواب أعني المسألة في ذهنه","english":"“It may be actual: either synoptically, by considering a simple item that is the principle of the details,” namely the details of its parts. Someone who knows an issue and is asked about it has the answer—that is, the issue—present in his mind—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I231.upper.01","editorial":"Ijmāl is translated “synoptically”: the text does not equate it with ignorance of the details, and the next page’s gloss debates whether it is one form or many.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I231.lower.01","volume":1,"page":231,"physical_pdf_page":244,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والفرق بينهما معلوم الخ) واعترض بأن الحاصل في العلم الإجمالي إما أن يكون\nصورة واحدة فيلزم أن يكون للحقائق المختلفة صورة واحدة مطابقة لكل منها\nمساوية لها ونفس ماهيتها وهو باطل وإما أن يكون صورا متعددة لتلك المختلفات\nفيكون العلم التفصيلي بها حاصلا فلا فرق وغاية التفرقة على ما هو ظاهر عبارتهم\nهي أن حصول الصور إن كان دفعة واحدة فعلم إجمالي وإن كان على ترتيب زماني\nبأن يحصل واحد بعد واحد فتفصيلي لكن حينئذ لا يكون العلم الإجمالي مرتبة\nمتوسطة بين القوة المحضة والفعل المحض مع أنه خلاف ما زعموه من توسطه بينهما\nويمكن الجواب بأن الحاصل في الإجمال صورة واحدة تطابق الكل لكن بالاجتماع\nمن غير ملاحظة تفاصيل الأجزاء وفي التفصيل صور متعددة يطابق كل منها واحدا\nمن الأجزاء على الانفراد والتفصيل كذا قالوا","english":"On “The difference is known, etc.” The objection is that what is obtained in synoptic knowledge is either one form or several. If one form, different realities must have one form corresponding to each of them, equal to each and identical with its quiddity; this is false. If several forms of the different realities, detailed knowledge of them is already present, and there is no difference. The greatest distinction apparent from their wording is that, if the forms occur simultaneously, knowledge is synoptic, while if they occur in temporal sequence, one after another, it is detailed. But synoptic knowledge would then not be an intermediate degree between pure potentiality and pure actuality, contrary to their claim that it stands between them. A possible reply is that synoptic knowledge contains one form corresponding to the whole, but collectively and without attention to the details of its parts; detailed knowledge contains several forms, each corresponding to one part individually and distinctly. So they have said.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I231.lower.02","volume":1,"page":231,"physical_pdf_page":244,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله على تجانس العلوم الخ) إن\nقيل التحقيق هو أن التصور والتصديق متخالفان بالذات قلنا لعله أراد بالعلم ما هو أحد\nأقسام التصديق أو أراد التصورات متماثلة وكذا التصديقات أو فقول هذا البعض\nمن الذين يرون أن حقيقة العلم مطلقا هي الصفة الموجبة لتمييز","english":"On “On the homogeneity of cognitions, etc.” If it is said that the verified position is that conception and assent differ intrinsically, we reply: perhaps by knowledge he means one of the divisions of assent; or he means that conceptions are homogeneous with one another and likewise assents; or this is the statement of those who regard the reality of knowledge without restriction as the attribute producing discrimination.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[{"locus":"أو فقول هذا البعض","reading":"أو فقول هذا البعض","status":"awkward printed syntax retained"}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I231.lower.03","volume":1,"page":231,"physical_pdf_page":244,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والجواز بالنظر الخ)","english":"On “Its possibility concerns, etc.”—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I232.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I231.upper.01","volume":1,"page":231,"physical_pdf_page":244,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"دفعة من غير تفصيل (أو تفصيلا بأن) ينظر إلى أجزاء المعلوم و(يلاحظ\nالتفاصيل) واحدا بعد واحد وشبه ذلك بمن يرى نعما كثيرة تارة دفعة فإنه يرى\nفي هذه الحالة جميعها ضرورة وتارة بأن يحدق البصر نحو واحد واحد ويميز ويفصل\nالأجزاء بعضها عن بعض فالرؤية الأولى إجمالية والثانية تفصيلية والفرق بينهما\nمعلوم بالوجدان فحال البصيرة بالنسبة إلى مدركاتها على مثال البصر بالنسبة\nإلى مدركاته في ثبوت الحالتين فيها أيضا","english":"[The answer is present] all at once, without differentiation; “or in detail, by” looking to the parts of the object of knowledge and “considering the details” one after another. This is compared to someone seeing many livestock: at one time all at once—necessarily seeing all of them in that state—and at another by directing his gaze towards each individually, distinguishing and separating their parts. The first seeing is synoptic, the second detailed; the difference is known through immediate experience. Intellectual insight’s relation to its objects is analogous to vision’s relation to its objects: these two states are found in it as well.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I230.upper.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I231.upper.02","volume":1,"page":231,"physical_pdf_page":244,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ويجوز) بالاتفاق (انقلاب) العلم\n(النظري ضروريا) فعندنا بأن يخلق الله تعالى في العبد علما ضروريا متعلقا\nبما تعلق به علمه النظري والمعتزلة اعتمدوا في الجواز على تجانس العلوم ومنعوا\nالوقوع فيما يكون مكلفا به كالعلم بالله وصفاته لئلا يلزم التكليف بغير المقدور وأنه\nقبيح يمتنع وقوعه من الله تعالى والجواز بالنظر إلى كونه علما وإنما الممتنع وقوعه","english":"“Theoretical” knowledge “may become necessary,” by agreement. On our account, this occurs when God creates in the servant a necessary cognition relating to what his theoretical cognition related to. The Muʿtazila based the possibility on the homogeneity of cognitions, but denied its occurrence where the cognition is an object of obligation, such as knowledge of God and His attributes. Otherwise an obligation concerning what is not within power would follow, which is bad and cannot proceed from God. Its possibility concerns the cognition insofar as it is knowledge; what is excluded is its actual occurrence—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I232.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.lower.01","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"جواب عما يقال أن اللازم مما ذكر هو نفي الجواز دون الوقوع وحاصله أنه\nليس معنى كلامهم الانقلاب في العلم بالله تعالى مثلا جائز وليس بواقع بل معناه أنه\nجائز نظرا إلى كونه علما","english":"[This] answers the objection that what the argument establishes is the denial of possibility, not merely of occurrence. In summary, their statement does not mean that conversion in knowledge of God, for example, is possible but does not occur. It means that it is possible when considered insofar as it is knowledge.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I231.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.lower.02","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بجواز أن يكون التنوع الخ) أقول ذكر\nالتنوع مشعر بأن مجوز الانقلاب أراد بالتجانس الاشتراك في الجنس على ما هو\nمصطلح الفلاسفة وليس كذلك فإنه من المتكلمين وفسره بالمماثلة والاتفاق في الماهية\nعلى ما في شرح المقاصد","english":"On “A difference of species may, etc.” I say: mentioning species difference suggests that the proponent of conversion meant by homogeneity a sharing of genus, in the philosophers’ terminology. This is not so: he is one of the theologians, and explains it as similarity and agreement in quiddity, according to Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid.","reading_status":"first_pass","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.lower.03","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهنا قول آخر الخ) فإن قلت هذا يشعر بأن\nالقول الأول هو الجواز مطلقا أي في كل ضروري سواء كان شرطا لكمال العقل\nأو لا وفساده ظاهر لاستحالة النظري بدون الضروري كما بين في محله قلنا ذلك إنما\nيمنع جواز اجتماع كل الأفراد على الانقلاب بحيث لا يبقى شيء من الضروريات\nلا جواز الانقلاب على كل فرد على الانفراد وهو المراد من القول الأول","english":"On “A further view holds, etc.” If you say: this suggests that the first view permits conversion without restriction, for every necessary cognition, whether or not it is a condition of intellectual perfection; its falsity is evident, since theoretical cognition without necessary cognition is impossible, as explained in its proper place—we reply: that rules out only the possibility of all instances being converted together, leaving no necessary cognition. It does not rule out conversion for each instance separately, which is what the first view means.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"The difference between “all together” and “each separately” is essential to the objection and reply.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.lower.04","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لأن\nكماله شرط للنظر الخ) لعله أراد مثل ما ذكره المصنف في شرح المقاصد بقوله\nوذهب إمام الحرمين إلى أنه لا يجوز في ضروري هو شرط لكمال العقل الذي به","english":"On “For its perfection is a condition of inquiry, etc.” Perhaps he intended something like what the author states in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: the Imām al-Ḥaramayn held that conversion is not possible for a necessary cognition that is a condition of the perfection of the intellect through which a person—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I233.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لتوقفه عليه الخ) وفيه أن المتوقف على النظر هو حصول النظري لا كونه\nنظريا ولا استحالة فيه اه منه","english":"On “Because it depends upon it, etc.” The objection is that what depends on inquiry is the occurrence of theoretical cognition, not its being theoretical; there is no impossibility in the latter. End. From him.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"This small ruled note criticises the upper argument. Its printed signature منه is retained without personal author attribution.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.upper.01","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لعارض التكليف به","english":"[What is excluded is its occurrence] because of the incidental circumstance of its being an object of obligation.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I231.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.upper.02","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وفي عكسه) أي انقلاب الضروري نظريا (خلاف)\nجوزه البعض لتجانس العلوم فيصح على كل ما يصح على الآخر ورد عليه بجواز أن\nيكون التنوع أو التشخص مانعا عن ذلك ومنعه آخرون لأنه يفضي إلى جواز الخلو\nعن الضروري المؤدي إلى جواز خلو العاقل الناظر في العلوم عن العلم باستحالة\nاجتماع الضدين وأن الكل أعظم من الجزء وغير ذلك من الضروريات التي تلزم\nالعاقل وأنه محال بالوجدان وفيه أن الخلو عن الضروري إنما يمتنع ما دام ضروريا\nوبعد الانقلاب لا يبقى هذا الوصف وهنا قول آخر أنه لا يجوز في ضروري هو\nشرط لكمال العقل لأن كماله شرط للنظر والنظر شرط للنظري لتوقفه عليه","english":"“Its converse,” necessary knowledge becoming theoretical, “is disputed.” Some allowed it because cognitions are homogeneous, so what is possible for one is possible for another. The objection is that a difference of species or individual determination may prevent this. Others denied it because it would allow the absence of necessary knowledge, and thus allow a rational person investigating cognitions to lack knowledge that contraries cannot coexist, that the whole is greater than the part, and other necessary truths inseparable from the rational person; such absence is impossible, as immediate experience attests. The reply is that absence of necessary cognition is impossible only so long as it is necessary; after its conversion, this description no longer applies. A further view holds that the conversion is impossible for a necessary cognition that is a condition of the perfection of intellect: the intellect’s perfection is a condition of inquiry, and inquiry is a condition of theoretical cognition because that cognition depends upon it.","reading_status":"first_pass","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Enlarged original establishes وأنه محال بالوجدان and وفيه أن الخلو عن الضروري إنما يمتنع. Earlier draft readings are superseded."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I232.upper.03","volume":1,"page":232,"physical_pdf_page":245,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(كما)","english":"“Just as”—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I233.upper.01","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Parenthesised كما joins اختلف on I:233: “Similarly, there is disagreement ...”."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I233.lower.01","volume":1,"page":233,"physical_pdf_page":246,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"يتأهل لاكتساب النظريات لأنه لو انقلب نظريا لزم كونه شرطا لنفسه\nوهو دور انتهى وأنت خبير بأنه نقل غير واف بهذا المقصود كما لا يخفى على المتدبر\nفي العبارتين ثم أقول إن من البين أنهم لم يريدوا من انقلاب العلمين أن يصير\nأحدهما بعينه هو الآخر فإنه من الانقلاب المستحيل بل المراد هو أنه يتعلق\nأحدهما بما يتعلق به الآخر على سبيل البدل وهذا ليس من المستحيل سواء كانا\nمتجانسين أو متخالفين سيما إذا كانا من شخصين فتدبر","english":"[The preceding account concerns that by which a person] becomes fitted to acquire theoretical cognitions; for, if it became theoretical, it would have to be a condition of itself, which is circular. End of quotation. You are aware that this quotation does not adequately establish the intended point, as reflection on the two formulations makes clear. I further say: it is evident that by the conversion of the two kinds of knowledge they did not intend that one numerical cognition becomes the other; that would be an impossible conversion. Rather, they intend that one kind relates, by substitution, to what the other kind relates to. This is not impossible, whether the two cognitions are homogeneous or different in kind, especially if they belong to two different persons. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I232.lower.04","readings":[],"proof_notes":["Enlargement establishes إن من البين and متجانسين أو متخالفين; no supplied referent remains necessary."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I233.lower.02","volume":1,"page":233,"physical_pdf_page":246,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ثم اختلفوا في أن العلم\nالضروري هل يجوز استناده إلى النظري أو لا فمنعه منهم متمسكا بأنه لو توقف على\nالنظر لزم توقفه على النظر فيكون نظريا لا ضروريا هذا خلف وجوزه بعض\nآخر متمسكا بأن العلم بامتناع اجتماع الضدين ضروري مع توقفه على النظر","english":"They then disagreed over whether necessary knowledge can depend on theoretical knowledge. One of them denied this, arguing that if it depended on inquiry, dependence on inquiry would follow, making it theoretical rather than necessary—a contradiction. Another allowed it, arguing that knowledge that two contraries cannot coexist is necessary, although it depends on examining—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I233.lower.03","editorial":"The repetitive wording of the first argument is preserved. The following unit completes the objection concerning examination of the contraries’ natures.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I233.lower.03","volume":1,"page":233,"physical_pdf_page":246,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"في حقيقتهما وأجيب بأن التوقف إنما هو على تصورهما بوجه ما وهو لا يلزم أن\nيكون بالنظر والحق أن هذا نزاع لفظي يرجع إلى تفسير التصديق الضروري هل\nهو الذي لا يفتقر إلى النظر أصلا لا في نفسه ولا في أطرافه أو هو الذي لا يفتقر\nإليه في نفسه فقط كذا حرره بعض المحققين","english":"[It depends on examining] their realities. The reply is that it depends only on conceiving them in some respect, which need not require inquiry. The truth is that this is a verbal disagreement turning on the explanation of necessary assent: is it that which needs no inquiry at all, either in itself or in its terms, or that which needs no inquiry only in itself? This is how one of the verifying scholars formulated it.","reading_status":"first_pass","join_in":"I233.lower.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I233.lower.04","volume":1,"page":233,"physical_pdf_page":246,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله الحادث الخ) بخلاف القديم\nفإنهم اتفقوا على أنه واحد يتعلق بمعلومات متعددة وأما الحادث فقد اختلفوا فيه\nفذهب الشيخ ومن تبعه إلى أن الواحد منه يمتنع أن يتعلق بمعلومين مثلا وهو\nالمعنى بقولهم تعدد العلم بتعدد المعلوم وقد يقال لو تعلق الواحد منه بما فوق الواحد\nلزم تعلقه بما لا نهاية له ولا يخفى ضعفه جدا وذهب بعض الأصحاب إلى أنه يجوز\nبل يجب واستدل عليه بأن العلم بالشيء يستلزم العلم بذلك وهكذا لا إلى نهاية\nفلو لم تكن عدة من هذه المعلومات معلومة بعلم واحد لزم حصول علوم غير متناهية\nلكل من علم شيئا واحدا ولا يخفى ضعف هذا أيضا","english":"On “Originated, etc.” Unlike eternal knowledge: they agree that it is one and relates to many objects of knowledge. They disagree, however, about originated knowledge. The Shaykh and his followers held that a single instance cannot relate, for example, to two objects. This is what is meant by their saying that knowledge is multiplied with the multiplication of its objects. It may be argued that if a single instance could relate to more than one object, it would have to relate to infinitely many; the extreme weakness of this argument is evident. Some of our companions held that such a relation is possible, indeed necessary. They argued that knowing a thing entails knowing that one knows it, and so on without end. If several of these objects were not known through one cognition, every person knowing a single thing would possess infinitely many cognitions. The weakness of this argument too is evident.","reading_status":"first_pass","editorial":"“The Shaykh” remains the source designation; it is not silently expanded into a personal identification.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I233.lower.05","volume":1,"page":233,"physical_pdf_page":246,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أو صفة ذات إضافة الخ)\nأورد عليه أن عدم الامتناع عند العقل بالنظر إلى كون العلم صفة ذات إضافة","english":"On “Or an attribute possessing a relation, etc.” The objection was raised that reason’s finding no impossibility, in view of knowledge’s being an attribute possessing a relation—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I234.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I233.upper.01","volume":1,"page":233,"physical_pdf_page":246,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"اختلف (في تعدد العلم) الحادث (بتعدد المعلوم) وبنى الإمام الرازي هذا\nالاختلاف على الخلاف في تفسير العلم أنه إضافة فيكون التعلق بهذا غير بذاك أو\nصفة ذات إضافة فيجوز للواحد تعلقات بأمور متعددة وكثرة التعلقات الخارجة","english":"There is disagreement “over whether” originated “knowledge is multiplied with the multiplication of what is known.” The Imām al-Rāzī based this disagreement on the dispute about knowledge’s definition. If knowledge is a relation, its relation to this thing differs from its relation to that thing. If it is an attribute possessing a relation, a single attribute may have relations to several things; the multiplicity of relations external—","reading_status":"first_pass","join_out":"I234.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false}]);